"""Original Security teaching packs. Each body is instructor prose."""

BODIES = {
    'Load balancing versus clustering': {
        'body': "Spreading incoming requests across several machines is a different job from tying machines together so one can take over the other's work. Both ideas add more than one box, and the exam stem counts on that similarity. The spreader does not, by itself, hand one machine's memory to the next. The takeover design does not, by itself, share a crowd of new visitors across every node.\n\nA load balancer presents one address and hands each new request to a healthy server. Least-connections and round-robin are common ways to choose. A health check pulls a dead server out of the rotation so clients stop landing on it. Session stickiness can pin a user to one server for a while, which is a convenience, not shared state. A cluster keeps nodes in a group with a heartbeat. When one node fails, another already holds the data or can mount it and continue. Quorum stops a split group from both claiming to be in charge. You may put a balancer in front of application nodes that are also clustered, and you still have two mechanisms to name.\n\nThe clinic web address 10.1.8.10 on port 443 lands on a balancer that spreads traffic across web01, web02, and web03. When web02 failed its health check during ticket NET-301, new chart requests went to the other two, and users with no saved session signed in again. The database pair is separate. Node db-a serves writes, db-b receives the data, and a heartbeat lets db-b take over. During the same ticket the team failed the database on purpose. The balancer did not perform that takeover. Calling the web trio a cluster because there are three of them would mislabel a spreader.\n\nDefenders draw the two boxes on different lines of the diagram. For the web tier, record the virtual address, the member list, and the health-check result. For the database, record which node is primary, how the heartbeat is monitored, and what a failover does to open transactions. Alert when a member is out of rotation for longer than the usual blip, and alert when the cluster heartbeat is lost. Do not fix a database failover by adding another web server, and do not fix an overloaded web tier by only adding a passive database node.\n\nStudents treat every pair of servers as a cluster, and they treat every cluster as a way to share a busy line. A stem that shows requests divided across web servers is the spreader. A stem that shows a second node taking over the data is the cluster. The separating fact is what moves. Requests move across the web members. The database role moves to the node that can continue the same data.\n",
        'r0': 'Load balancing spreads requests across members behind one address. Clustering uses a heartbeat so a peer can take over state. Clinic web clients use 10.1.8.10 port 443, and least-connections sends them to web01, web02, or web03 until a health check removes a sick member. Stickiness pins a session and does not copy memory between nodes. The database pair db-a and db-b is the cluster in NET-301: db-b already holds the data and can become primary. Quorum prevents two sides from both serving writes. A balancer may sit in front of clustered app nodes and remains a second mechanism. Name the spreader when requests are divided. Name the cluster when the data role moves.\n',
        'r1': 'Sharing new requests across machines is one job. A partner that continues the same work is another job. A spreader does not hand over memory by itself. A health check drops a dead machine from the line. A heartbeat lets a second database step in. You may put a spreader in front of a group. The two ideas stay different. A busy web line is the spreader story. A database that can take over is the partner story. Do not swap the names just because both add hardware. Ask whether requests move or the data role moves.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a clinic cafeteria with two serving lines and one cook who can finish a tray the other cook started. The two lines split the crowd so neither window faces every hungry person alone. The second cook is there so a tray already in progress can be finished if the first cook steps out. Splitting the crowd does not teach the second cook what was on a half-made tray. A cook who can finish the tray is not the same thing as opening a second window. In the clinic design, the web address that shares visitors is the pair of serving lines. The database node that continues the same records is the cook who can finish the tray.\n',
        'r4': 'On NET-301, write the virtual address, the web members, and the health check apart from the database pair. Note that web02 left rotation while db-b took the data role. Do not call the three web servers a cluster on that evidence alone. Do not call the database pair a request spreader. Record heartbeat loss separately from a failed web health check. Stop when the ticket says which design moves requests and which design moves the data role.\n',
        'exam': 'Divided web requests are load balancing. A second node that takes over the data is clustering. Three web servers are not a cluster merely because they share a name. A heartbeat failover is not proof that new visitors are being spread. Stickiness is not shared memory. You can use both designs in one application and still name them separately. The question to ask is what moves: the next request, or the data role.\n',
    },
    'Hot sites': {
        'body': "A hot site is ready to run now. Systems are already in place, and the data is current or only moments behind, so the switch is measured in minutes rather than in a rebuild. You pay for that speed with a second live environment. The point of the spend is a short recovery time, not a cheaper contract.\n\nThe spare is already powered and already receiving copies, so you do not wait to install equipment or load yesterday's full backup before work can resume. Replication keeps the spare close to production. A drill should actually move a real task, such as opening a chart, and record how long users were quiet. People still decide to invoke the site. They should not be waiting on racks, data loads, or a hiring call. A warm site is the neighbor students reach for: equipment is there, and it still needs data or staff before it can carry the day. A cold site is further away from ready. It is space with little equipment.\n\nThe clinic keeps a second environment that receives database copies with a lag around 45 seconds. During drill DR-HOT-04 the operations lead declared the switch, and test users opened charts on the spare in 8 minutes, inside a 15-minute target. The spare was not an empty room, and it was not a rack waiting for tapes. It was already running. The invoice for that readiness is a full second stack of compute and storage, which is why leadership signed it against the payroll downtime they refuse to accept. A design that still needs a four-hour restore is a different label, even if the vendor brochure says hot.\n\nDefenders time the switch, check replication lag on ordinary days, and refuse to call the site hot when lag has grown to hours. Confirm staffing for the declaration is already named, because a ready site with nobody allowed to invoke it does not meet the promise. Test the application, not only the virtual machine power light. Keep the runbook at the spare as well as at the primary so the team that travels, or the team already there, is not reading from a server that is down.\n\nStudents label any rented space a hot site. Equipment without current data is not ready to run now. Space with little equipment is not ready to run now. A disk mirror inside the primary building is not a second site at all. The separating fact is readiness at the moment of the disaster. If a drill can move real work in minutes onto a system that was already current, you are looking at a hot site.\n",
        'r0': 'A hot site is ready to run now, with current or near-current data, so the switch is fast and the cost is high. DR-HOT-04 moved chart access in 8 minutes against a 15-minute target, with replication lag around 45 seconds. That is a short recovery time, not a rebuild. A warm site has equipment and still needs data or staff before it carries the full load. A cold site is space with little equipment. A disk mirror in the same building is not a second site. Time a real user task during the drill, and watch lag on ordinary days. Hours of lag means you should stop calling the spare hot. Evidence is a timed switch onto data that was already current.\n',
        'r1': 'A hot site is ready to run now. The data is already there or moments behind. The switch is fast, and the bill is high. You should not be installing servers during the disaster. You should not be loading yesterday from scratch. Equipment that still needs data or people is a slower site. A nearly empty room is a colder site. A mirror inside the same building is not a second site. Time a real task when you practice the switch. If the practice takes hours, stop calling the site hot.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a second clinic wing with the lights already on, the charts already copied, and a desk ready for the next patient. You walk across the corridor and continue the visit. You do not spend the morning moving beds in or calling staff from home before the first patient can be seen. A wing that has beds but no charts yet makes you wait. An empty leased bay makes you wait much longer. In the recovery design, the spare that served charts in minutes during the drill is that lit wing. If the charts on the spare are hours old, the wing is no longer the ready one you paid for.\n',
        'r4': 'Read DR-HOT-04 and record the 8-minute chart switch and the lag figure. Compare that time with the 15-minute target before you keep the hot label. Note whether users completed a real task, not only whether machines powered on. Do not apply this label if the spare still needs a data load or a staff call-in. Do not apply it to an empty room. Stop when the ticket shows a spare that was already current and a switch measured in minutes.\n',
        'exam': 'Ready to run now is the hot site. Current or near-current data is part of that readiness. A warm site has equipment and still needs data or staff. A cold site is space with little equipment. A same-building disk mirror is not a hot site. A brochure that says hot does not override a multi-hour restore. The stem to recognize is a timed switch, in minutes, onto a spare that was already up to date.\n',
    },
    'Warm sites': {
        'body': 'Warm sites keep equipment racked and powered, plus some data, and still need a restore or a catch-up before full service. Warm sites plan the move in hours, not minutes, which is the usual recovery time once the catch-up starts. Warm sites often hold last night\'s copy, so the recovery point objective is about a day unless extra logs arrive. Warm sites cost less than hot sites because the spare is not fully live every ordinary hour. Warm sites recover faster than cold sites because the team is not waiting for hardware to be delivered and installed. Warm sites show their gap in the restore clock: loading data, replaying changes, and checking the application. Warm sites must name which backup, snapshot, or log stream will close that gap. Warm sites fail the promise if the spare disk is too small for the restored set. Warm sites fail the promise if the only copy lives in the same building that just flooded. Warm sites need a runbook that lists restore order, including identity, databases, and the applications that depend on them. Warm sites need people who have practiced that restore, not a binder nobody has opened. Warm sites differ from load balancing, which spreads today\'s traffic and does not rebuild yesterday\'s data. Warm sites differ from a hot facility that is already serving on near-live replication. Warm sites set expectations with the business in hours of downtime and in hours of lost work. Warm sites test the catch-up on a schedule and write the actual hours beside the hoped-for hours. Warm sites include network changes, name service, and partner links in that timed test. Warm sites confirm licenses allow the spare to run once, even if it is quiet most days. Warm sites keep the spare patched on a planned cadence so the restore does not land on an ancient image. Warm sites protect the stored copy with encryption and with a key the spare team can reach. Warm sites avoid storing the only key in the building you are trying to survive. Warm sites document what "good enough" service looks like while the catch-up is still running. Warm sites may start with read-only service or a reduced menu while the last logs replay. Warm sites record dependencies such as print servers and interface engines that a database-only restore forgets. Warm sites compare the spare\'s memory and core count with production so a restored system is not instantly overloaded. Warm sites revisit the design when the business asks for a minute-level switch, because this pattern will not meet it. Warm sites keep a path to upgrade toward a hotter design if the recovery time objective shrinks. Warm sites separate this label from geographic distance: a warm room next door still dies in the same flood. Warm sites separate this label from platform diversity: matching hardware does not fix a single-vendor bug. Warm sites log who may start the restore and who may send users to the spare. Warm sites capture, after a real event, how long the catch-up truly took and correct the next plan. Warm sites list the order of databases, identity, and dependent applications before the first hour of an event. Warm sites store the catch-up media where a failure of the primary building does not take it too. Warm sites name a deputy who can run the restore if the primary operator is unavailable.',
        'r0': 'Warm sites stage racked systems and a partial data set, then budget hours for restore, replay, and application checks. Warm sites derive the recovery point objective from the age of that data set, often the previous night plus any journals. Warm sites sit between a near-live hot facility and a cold room that still lacks computers. Warm sites require spare capacity large enough for the full restored working set, not a demo slice. Warm sites time a full catch-up drill and store the measured hours next to the published recovery time objective. Warm sites refuse the label when the stem shows continuous replication and a minute-scale switch. Warm sites publish the restore order and a deputy operator before calling the hours estimate real.',
        'r1': "Warm sites have gear plus some data. Warm sites still need a restore or a catch-up. Warm sites plan in hours, not minutes. Warm sites often start from last night's copy. Warm sites cost less than a live spare. Warm sites finish sooner than an empty room. Warm sites must fit the restored data on disk. Warm sites need a practiced restore order. Warm sites are not a traffic spreader. Warm sites are not an already-live replica. Warm sites need a deputy who can restore.",
        'r2': "Warm sites resemble a second kitchen whose ovens are installed and whose cooler holds yesterday's dough, not today's soup. Warm sites ask the crew to bake before the doors open, which takes hours rather than a quick walk next door. Warm sites cost less than a kitchen that cooks the same menu all day, and they beat an empty stall. Warm sites fail if the cooler is too small for the full menu or if the dough was stored in the flooded shop. Warm sites need bakers who have rehearsed that morning bake, not a recipe card still in the plastic. Warm sites are not a longer serving counter, and they are not a pot that is already hot.",
        'r4': "Time Warm sites by restoring last night's copy and writing the actual hours down. Compare Warm sites with a hot facility before you promise a minute-level switch. Measure Warm sites disk and memory against the full working set. Require Warm sites restore order to include identity, data, and dependent applications. Encrypt Warm sites copies and store the key where the spare crew can reach it. Reject Warm sites as the label when replication is already near-live.",
        'exam': "Warm sites is the label when hardware is waiting and yesterday's copy still has to be loaded, with the clock in hours. Warm sites is not the label for an already-live replica, and not the label for an empty room. Warm sites uses the age of the spare data as the clue to the recovery point objective. Warm sites fails the stem when the spare cannot hold the restored set or nobody has practiced the order.",
    },
    'Cold sites': {
        'body': "A cold site is space with little equipment. Power and a secure room may be waiting, and the servers, the data, and often the people are not. It is the cheap end of recovery planning, and it is slow. An empty cage will not carry Monday morning appointments. You still have to move in.\n\nThe contract usually buys floor space, power, cooling, and a way in. It does not buy a running copy of the clinic application. After a disaster you ship or purchase hardware, restore backups, and bring staff. That sequence is why the recovery time is long, sometimes measured in days. The backups still have to exist somewhere else, because the cold room does not contain them. Calling the room a plan, without a tested restore onto equipment you can actually obtain, is how this option gets oversold. Cost is the reason it appears in a budget. Time is the reason it fails a short recovery target.\n\nThe clinic's cold option is a cage at a hosting building with power, a badge, and no production racks. The contract language says the clinic must supply the systems. Ticket DR-COLD-02 records a planning review that timed a realistic move-in at about three days before a single file server could be restored, which misses a Monday outage completely. Nothing in that cage was receiving live copies. A hot site, by contrast, is ready to run now. A warm site has equipment and still needs data or staff. The cage matches neither of those. It matches an empty place you move into.\n\nDefenders write the recovery time next to the cold-site contract so nobody confuses low rent with a short outage. List what must be delivered: hardware, backup media or cloud copies, passwords, and people. If the business cannot tolerate days of downtime, this site cannot be the only answer for that service. Review the contract for power and access, and still do not describe those utilities as a running clinic. Pair the room with backups that were actually restored in a test, or the room is a promise sitting on top of an unread tape.\n\nStudents hear site and picture a ready data center. Space is not a hot site, and it is not a warm site with racks waiting. Students also think a cold site replaces backups. You bring the data with you. The separating fact is how little is already there. If the stem describes an empty room or a cage with power and little equipment, and the team must haul systems in, you are looking at a cold site.\n",
        'r0': 'A cold site is space with little equipment, so you bring the systems and the data. DR-COLD-02 is a cage with power and a badge, and no production racks. A realistic move-in was about three days before one file server could be restored, which misses a Monday target. A hot site is ready to run now. A warm site has equipment and still needs data or staff. Cheap rent is not a short recovery time. Backups are still required, because the room does not hold the data. Access and power are not a running application. Evidence is an empty or nearly empty place plus a long build-out before users work.\n',
        'r1': 'A cold site is space with little equipment. You still have to move the systems in. You still have to bring the data. It costs less because almost nothing is running. It is too slow for a Monday morning outage. Power in the room is not a clinic system. A ready spare is a different, costly idea. Equipment that still needs data is a middle idea. Do not skip backups because a room is rented. Read the contract for what is actually on the floor.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture an empty market stall with a power outlet and a lock on the shutter. On a quiet day the stall looks like a plan. On the morning you need to sell, you still haul the stove, the food, and the people. The outlet does not cook. A second stall that already has a warm stove is a different purchase. A stall with tables in place but no food yet is a different purchase too. In the recovery contract, the cage with power and no racks is that empty stall. The three-day move-in is the morning you discover the outlet was never a clinic.\n',
        'r4': 'Open DR-COLD-02 and list what is physically in the cage. Confirm the contract makes the clinic supply the systems. Write the estimated days before a restore can finish. Do not describe power and a badge as a running application. Keep hot-site and warm-site notes from being copied onto this ticket. Confirm backups exist outside the cage. Stop when the ticket states space, little equipment, and a long move-in.\n',
        'exam': 'Space with little equipment is a cold site. Ready to run now is a hot site. Equipment that still needs data or staff is a warm site. Low cost does not mean a short outage. A rented room does not replace backups. Power and cooling are not a restored application. The stem to recognize is an empty or nearly empty place you must move into before anyone can work.\n',
    },
    'Geographic dispersion': {
        'body': "Geographic dispersion places copies far enough apart that one fire, flood, or regional outage does not take both. Distance is doing the protective work. Two copies in the same building, or in the building next door on the same power grid, fail together when the hazard is the place itself.\n\nFar enough is a hazard question, not a mileage trophy. Separate power grids, separate flood plains, and a distance that a single storm does not cover are the practical tests. A second availability area across the continent usually qualifies. A second rack across the server-room aisle does not. The copies still have to be real: data that is current enough for your recovery point, and a way to run. Dispersion without a usable copy is only a pin on a map. Dispersion is also not a second brand of software, and it is not automatically a second cloud company. Those are neighboring designs you may add. This one is about place.\n\nThe clinic primary sits in the eastern region. The recovery copy sits in a western region, on a different power grid, far enough that the eastern flood plain does not include it. Ticket ARCH-GEO-3 records the choice after a review noticed the previous spare was in the building next door, fed by the same utility. A building fire drill discussion had wrongly treated that neighbor as dispersed. Moving the copy west is what changed the hazard. The western copy still needs its own test. A pin on a map does not prove the data arrived.\n\nDefenders write the hazards they are separating: fire, flood, power, and a regional network cut. Then they check that the spare is outside each one. Ask the data-center or cloud region which grid and which metro it uses before accepting a salesperson's city name. Replicate or back up on a schedule that matches the recovery point, because a distant empty folder is not dispersion of the service. Review contracts that quietly place both copies in one metro for latency reasons. Latency is a real constraint. It does not erase a shared flood plain.\n\nStudents call two rooms in one building dispersed because the rooms have different numbers. Students also answer with a second cloud vendor when the stem only moved regions inside one provider, or they answer with distance when the stem is about a second software product. The separating fact is shared fate of a place. If one fire, flood, or grid failure would destroy both copies, you do not have this control yet, no matter how many servers you counted.\n",
        'r0': 'Geographic dispersion puts copies far enough apart that one fire, flood, or regional outage does not take both. ARCH-GEO-3 moved the spare from the building next door, on the same utility, to a western region on a different power grid. A second rack in the same room fails the test. A different flood plain and a different grid are the checks that matter more than a marketing city name. Distance is not a second software product and not, by itself, a second cloud company. The distant copy still has to receive data and be tested. A shared metro for latency can undo the hazard split. Evidence is two copies that do not share the disaster you wrote down.\n',
        'r1': 'Put copies far enough apart that one disaster misses one of them. The next building can share the power and the flood. A second rack in the same room is not far. Ask which hazards you are splitting. A different power grid matters. A different flood plain matters. A dot on a map is not a copy that works. A second brand of software is a different idea. A second cloud company is a different idea. Check that one fire would not take both.\n',
        'r2': "Picture two pantry closets. One sits in the clinic break room. The other sits at a cousin's house across the state, on a different street and a different power line. A kitchen fire eats the break-room closet and leaves the distant one. A spare shelf in the same break room burns with the first. A second brand of flour in the same closet is a useful idea and does not move the food out of the fire. In the clinic plan, the western region is the cousin's house. The building next door was only a second shelf in the same kitchen.\n",
        'r4': 'On ARCH-GEO-3, name the primary region and the spare region. Write the power grid and flood exposure for each. Reject a spare in the next building on the same utility if the hazard is that utility. Do not mark the map pin complete until data actually arrives at the far site. Keep a second software product in a different note. Keep a second provider in a different note. Stop when one listed disaster cannot reach both copies.\n',
        'exam': 'Separation by place is this control. The next building on the same grid is not enough. A second rack in the same room is not enough. A second product does not answer a flood question. A second provider is a different design unless the stem is about distance and hazards. A map pin without a copy is not dispersion. The stem to recognize is one fire, flood, or outage that would otherwise take both copies.\n',
    },
    'Platform diversity': {
        'body': "Platform diversity keeps a single product defect from knocking out every copy at once. You run the same kind of job on more than one implementation, so a bug, a bad patch, or a vendor outage tied to that product has somewhere else to go. Two copies of the identical software on two servers still share the bug.\n\nThe mechanism is a second design, not a second address. Name resolvers are the clearest clinic example: if every lookup depends on one vendor's code, a flaw in that code blanks the directory, the mail, and the application together. A second resolver from a different product can answer while the first is pulled. The same pattern fits hypervisors, load-balancer software, or authentication clients when the impact of one bug is wide. Diversity costs skill and spare capacity. It is wasted if both platforms are misconfigured in the same way, or if the backup platform has never answered a real query. It is also not the same as putting one product in two cities. A bad patch follows the product across the map.\n\nThe clinic runs name service on BIND for one resolver pair and on Windows DNS for another. Ticket ARCH-DNS-7 records a lab patch that crashed the BIND pair and left the Windows resolvers answering. Workstations that could use either path kept resolving clinic names. Workstations pinned only to BIND failed until the pin was lifted. That result is the control working, and it is also a finding about clients that knew only one platform. The ticket does not claim the two resolvers sit in different regions. They might. Place is a separate decision.\n\nDefenders list functions where one vendor bug would stop the clinic, and they put a real second implementation under at least one of them. Test the second path by disabling the first in a lab, not by assuming the config is symmetric. Patch one platform at a time so a bad update cannot land everywhere in the same hour. Watch for hidden single products, such as one virtualization stack under both resolvers, that reunite the fate you thought you split. Document which clients know how to use the spare platform.\n\nStudents answer with a second data center when the stem describes one bug killing every identical copy. Students answer with a second cloud company for the same story. Distance and a second provider can be wise and still leave you on one product. The separating fact is the implementation. If a defect in a single product would blank every copy, you do not have platform diversity yet, even if the pins on the map look far apart.\n",
        'r0': 'Platform diversity uses more than one product so a single bug does not blank every copy. ARCH-DNS-7 runs BIND beside Windows DNS. A lab patch crashed the BIND pair and the Windows resolvers still answered. Clients pinned only to BIND failed until that pin changed. Clients must be able to query the surviving product. Two copies of one product share the defect, including copies in two regions. A second cloud provider running the same software shares it too. Patch one platform at a time. A shared hypervisor under both resolvers can undo the split. Evidence is a working second implementation while the first product is broken.\n',
        'r1': 'Use more than one product so one bug does not stop everything. Two copies of the same software still share the bug. A second city does not fix a bad patch. A second cloud company does not fix a shared bug. The same software still fails together. Name lookup is a common place to split products. Test by turning the first product off. Patch one side at a time. Make sure clients can actually use the spare. A hidden shared layer can glue the two sides back together.\n',
        'r2': "Picture a bakery that buys flour from two mills. When one mill ships a bad batch, the other mill's sacks keep the ovens going. Two storerooms full of the same mill's flour fail together. A storeroom across town full of that same flour fails with them. A second grocery account that only sells the same brand is not a second mill. A shared oven model in both shops still stops when that model is recalled. In the clinic, BIND and the Windows resolver are the two mills. The lab crash of one pair, while the other still answered, is the bad batch that did not close the bakery.\n",
        'r4': 'On ARCH-DNS-7, name the two resolver products. Record which one failed in the lab and which one still answered. List clients that can use only one of them. Do not treat a second region of the same product as this control. Do not treat a second provider of the same product as this control. Check for a shared layer underneath both. Stop when the ticket shows one product broken and a different product still doing the job.\n',
        'exam': 'A second implementation is this control. A second copy of the same product is not. A second city does not remove a shared bug. A second provider does not remove it if the software stays the same. Clients that cannot use the spare undo the design. Patching every copy in the same hour recreates a single fate. The stem to recognize is one product defect that would otherwise blank every path.\n',
    },
    'Multi-cloud recovery': {
        'body': "Multi-cloud recovery means you can run the service in more than one cloud provider, and you have proved the move. One provider's outage should not be the end of the public site. An account you never used, or a diagram with a second logo, is not a recovery path.\n\nThe mechanism is a runbook plus data the second provider can actually see. Images, configuration, secrets, and a recent copy of the data have to exist outside the first provider's failure domain. Network names have to be able to point at the spare. People have to have credentials that work when the first console is down. A second region inside the same provider protects you from a building or a zone, and it does not protect you from a provider-wide control-plane failure. That is why this design names two companies, not two halls in one company. The test matters more than the contract. If the last proof is a slide, you do not yet know the path works.\n\nThe clinic's public site runs in the primary provider. Runbook CLOUD-REC-2 brings it up in the second provider. Last quarter the team did that in a planned window, served a test page, and wrote the elapsed time on ticket DR-MC-5. The data copy used for the test was already stored where the second provider could read it. An earlier draft had left backups only in the primary provider's storage, which would have been unreachable in the outage the design claims to survive. The ticket calls that draft a miss. The successful test is the evidence.\n\nDefenders schedule a real move, even a small one, and store the secrets and the data copy outside the provider you might lose. Check that DNS or the traffic director can be changed by people who are not locked inside the failed console. Review identity: if the only administrator login is a service of the primary provider, you can lose the keys and the building together. Cost and skill are real objections. They do not turn an untested logo into a recovery capability. After each test, fix the step that stalled before you declare the runbook current.\n\nStudents answer multi-cloud when the stem only shows two regions of one provider. That split can be geographic dispersion, and it is not a second provider. Students also count two contracts with no test. The separating fact is a proved path into another provider. If the public site has actually been stood up in a second provider, as DR-MC-5 records, you have this recovery. If you only have a logo on a slide, you have a proposal.\n",
        'r0': 'Multi-cloud recovery is a tested way to run in more than one provider so one provider outage is not the end. CLOUD-REC-2 stood the public site up in the second provider, and DR-MC-5 records that test. Data, images, and secrets must be readable outside the failed provider. Two regions of one provider do not answer a provider-wide failure. A second logo without a test is not a path. Administrator logins must work when the primary console is down. DNS or the traffic director has to be movable from outside the failure. Evidence is a service that actually ran in the second provider.\n',
        'r1': "Keep a real way to run in a second cloud company. One company's outage should not end the service. A picture of a second logo is not a path. A region pair inside one company is not a second company. The data has to be readable from the spare. The keys have to work when the first console is down. Practice the move or you do not know it works. Write down how long the practice took. Fix the step that stalled before you trust the instructions.\n",
        'r2': 'Picture a clinic that can sell flu shots at a second market if the first market floods. The second market only helps if the vials, the cooler, and the cashier key are already somewhere that flood cannot reach, and if someone has actually set up a table there once. A second stall in the same flooded market is a different plan. A brochure that names the second market, with no table ever set, is only a brochure. In the hosting design, the second provider is that other market. The quarter when the test page actually ran is the day the table was set, and a backup trapped in the first market would have left the table empty.\n',
        'r4': 'Open DR-MC-5 and confirm the public site ran in the second provider. Note where the data copy lived during that test. Confirm secrets and DNS changes do not depend on the primary console. Do not count two regions of one provider as this result. Do not count a diagram alone. Record the elapsed time and the step that stalled. Stop when the ticket shows a move that actually happened.\n',
        'exam': 'A tested path in a second provider is this recovery. Two regions of one provider are not. A contract or a logo without a test is not. Backups that live only inside the failed provider cannot support the move. Logins that live only there cannot either. Geographic distance inside one company is a different control. The stem to recognize is a service that was actually brought up somewhere else.\n',
    },
    'Continuity of operations': {
        'body': 'Continuity of operations is how the work continues when the usual place or the usual systems do not. It names who decides, who still works, and what good enough looks like for the mission. A server that boots again is part of recovery. It is not the whole answer if patients still cannot be seen.\n\nThe plan covers the business of the day. Succession says who is in charge if the usual lead is unreachable. Essential jobs are listed so people know which work waits and which work changes form. Alternate ways of working are written in advance: phone visits, paper packets, a sister clinic, a manual log. Good enough is explicit, because a full imaging schedule may be impossible while medication refills and urgent calls are not. Communications tell staff and patients which mode is active. The plan is practiced. A binder that nobody has opened will not be remembered at 06:00 when the building is closed.\n\nWhen the clinic building is unusable, appointments move to phone visits and the paper downtime packet DP-CLINIC is already printed at the nursing stations and at the answering service. Ticket COOP-11 records an exercise where the operations chief, not the server administrator, declared that mode and the front desk used the packet to rebuild the morning list. The electronic chart was down. Care did not stop. The packet includes who may give advice, how to record a call, and which cases must be sent to an open emergency department. That is the mission continuing at a reduced level. Restoring the chart is still required. It is a different work stream from deciding how the morning runs.\n\nDefenders keep the packet current, name the decision maker, and practice the declaration. Check that home numbers and the answering service script match the packet. Do not assume the only continuity action is a failover to a spare site. If the spare site is up and the phone script is wrong, patients still hear silence. After an exercise, fix the gap the exercise found, such as a packet that still lists a retired chief. Store a copy of the packet where the building outage does not destroy every copy.\n\nStudents equate this plan with a failover test or with a backup restore. Those prove systems. This plan proves the organization can still do its job, including the hours when systems are not back. Students also think any IT disaster document covers who speaks for the clinic. The separating fact is the mission. If the stem shows work continuing by an alternate method, with a named decision and a definition of good enough, you are looking at continuity of operations.\n',
        'r0': 'Continuity of operations keeps the mission going: who decides, who works, and what good enough means. COOP-11 closed the building and moved appointments to phone visits using printed packet DP-CLINIC. The operations chief declared the mode. The server administrator did not. The packet says how to log a call and which cases go to an open emergency department. Restoring servers is a related stream and does not replace the packet. A failover test proves a system move. This plan proves care can continue while the chart is down. Practice the declaration and refresh names. Evidence is an alternate way of working that people actually used.\n',
        'r1': 'This plan is how the job continues. A server reboot is only part of it. Name who is allowed to decide. Name which work still happens. Say what good enough looks like today. Phone visits can replace a closed building. A paper packet can carry the morning list. The person who reboots machines may not be the decider. Practice the switch before the morning it happens. A spare computer does not speak to patients by itself. Update the packet when leaders change.\n',
        'r2': "Picture a school that loses the classroom building and moves lessons to a gym with a paper roster already in the principal's bag. The roster says who teaches, which classes still meet, and which activities wait. A working server in a closet does not, by itself, tell parents where to send the children. A meeting that only talks about the gym, without ever opening the roster, leaves the bag untested. In the clinic, the phone-visit mode and the printed packet are that gym and that roster. The operations chief declaring the mode is the principal, not the person who would restart a server.\n",
        'r4': 'Read COOP-11 and name the person who declared the phone-visit mode. Confirm packet DP-CLINIC was already printed and say where copies live. List what good enough includes and what waits. Do not treat a server restore as the whole plan. Do not treat a failover timer as this declaration. Update any retired name in the packet. Stop when the ticket shows who decides, how work continues, and what good enough means.\n',
        'exam': 'How the mission continues is this plan. A server restore is not the whole plan. A failover timer is not the declaration. Who decides belongs in the answer. What good enough means belongs in the answer. A paper downtime method can be the continuity path while systems are down. The stem to recognize is work going on by an alternate method, not merely a machine powering back up.\n',
    },
    'Capacity planning': {
        'body': "Capacity planning asks whether people, technology, and space can carry the load you are promising, including the load during a failover. A spare that looks ready and cannot hold the work is a smaller copy, not a recovery. The promise in the plan and the size of the spare have to match.\n\nYou measure the real load, then you compare it with the spare. Disk, CPU, memory, and network are the technology side. Seats, phones, and a room that can hold the downtime staff are the space side. Roster depth is the people side: a weekend cutover that needs four database operators does not succeed with two. Peak days matter more than a quiet average. A failover adds its own load, because replication catch-up, restores, and confused users arrive together. A primary that is tuned for Tuesday does not answer whether the spare can live through a Monday disaster. Power and cooling are part of capacity when the spare site cannot feed the racks you intend to run.\n\nProduction file storage for the clinic is 16 TB used, with growth that fills a 20 TB volume in the planning window. The spare site volume is 8 TB. Ticket CAP-19 shows a full failover would fill that spare on day one, before the week of scans arrives. The same review found six seats in the spare workroom and a downtime plan that calls for twenty clerks, and a weekend database roster of two people against a procedure that lists four. None of those gaps is a software bug. Each one means the promised load does not fit. Buying a faster primary disk would not have fixed the spare.\n\nDefenders put the spare's numbers next to production numbers and next to the downtime roster. Re-measure after a big data project, because last year's 8 TB gap may now be larger. Do not accept a hot-site label, or any failover promise, while the spare cannot store the data or seat the people. Include the network path into the spare, not only the disk. Write the shortfall as a capacity gap with an owner, and do not close it by lowering the promised load in a footnote nobody sees.\n\nStudents treat capacity as a performance tweak on the live system. Tuning the primary is useful and is not this comparison. Students also answer with a backup schedule when the stem says the spare disk is half of production. Backups do not enlarge the spare. The separating fact is fit. If the spare cannot hold the people, the data, or the traffic you promised, the plan is oversized relative to the place you would land.\n",
        'r0': 'Capacity planning checks people, technology, and space against the load you promise, including failover. CAP-19 finds production data at 16 TB headed for a 20 TB volume, and a spare volume of 8 TB that would fill on day one. The spare workroom has six seats against a plan for twenty clerks. The weekend database roster has two people against a procedure that needs four. Tuning the primary does not enlarge the spare. A backup schedule does not either. Re-measure after large data projects. A short recovery promise is false if the spare cannot hold the day. Evidence is the side-by-side shortfall during failover.\n',
        'r1': 'Ask whether the spare can carry the load you promised. Count machines, room, and people. A disk half the size of production fills too soon. Six chairs cannot seat twenty clerks. Two people cannot do a job that needs four. A faster main system does not fix the spare. A backup schedule does not make the spare bigger. Include the busy day, not only a quiet average. Measure again after you store more data. Do not promise a move onto a place that is too small.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a spare dining room kept for the Saturday crowd. The main room seats the rush. The spare room has four chairs and a short table. Moving Saturday into that room does not become possible because the main oven was tuned. A notebook of recipes does not add chairs. A second seating time does not fix a room that cannot hold the guests you promised. In the clinic failover, the 8 TB spare against a much larger production set is that short table. The six seats against twenty clerks are the missing chairs, and day one is the Saturday you finally notice.\n',
        'r4': 'On CAP-19, write production size beside the spare size. Record 16 TB growing toward 20 TB against the 8 TB spare. Record six seats against twenty clerks and two operators against four. Do not close the gap by tuning only the primary. Do not cite a backup schedule as extra spare disk. Name an owner for the shortfall. Stop when the ticket shows whether failover capacity can hold the promised load.\n',
        'exam': "Fit during failover is this planning. A half-size spare disk is a capacity miss. Too few seats or too few people is the same miss. Tuning the primary is not the spare's capacity. A backup schedule does not enlarge the spare. A quiet-day average can hide the peak you must carry. The stem to recognize is a promised load the spare cannot hold on day one.\n",
    },
    'Tabletop exercises': {
        'body': 'Tabletop exercises gather the right roles to narrate an incident and expose gaps while production stays untouched. Tabletop exercises keep systems up on purpose, which is why they cannot prove that a replica will actually boot. Tabletop exercises surface missing decisions, such as who may close a service or who calls affected customers. Tabletop exercises include the right roles: operations, the service owner, communications, and the person with spending authority. Tabletop exercises use a scenario written in advance, with injects that force a choice every few minutes. Tabletop exercises record each gap in plain language and assign an owner and a date. Tabletop exercises differ from failover tests, which actually move the work and time the quiet period. Tabletop exercises differ from simulation tests, which inject signals and watch detection and response tooling. Tabletop exercises work well for continuity questions, legal questions, and vendor-call questions. Tabletop exercises work poorly as the only evidence that a backup can be restored. Tabletop exercises should be short enough that busy people will attend, and specific enough that they cannot hide. Tabletop exercises name a facilitator who is not also playing every role. Tabletop exercises collect the downtime packet and the call tree and see whether anyone can find them. Tabletop exercises walk a building closure, a lost admin, or a bad public update without touching live keys. Tabletop exercises avoid turning the hour into a blame session, or the next gap stays hidden. Tabletop exercises end with a short list of changes, not a novel. Tabletop exercises retest the biggest gap in a later session to see if the fix is real. Tabletop exercises invite a deputy, because the primary expert will be on vacation during a real event. Tabletop exercises include a supplier or a building contact if the scenario needs them. Tabletop exercises do not claim a passed tabletop means the hot site works. Tabletop exercises do not skip the tabletop because a technical test exists, since authority gaps survive a successful boot. Tabletop exercises store the notes where the next facilitator can find them. Tabletop exercises tie each gap to a control you will change: a name in a plan, a pre-printed form, a license. Tabletop exercises schedule the next one before the group leaves the room. Tabletop exercises use a clock so the discussion cannot wander past the decisions. Tabletop exercises ask what evidence would tell the group the event is over. Tabletop exercises ask what would be true if the first decision maker cannot be reached. Tabletop exercises match the ticket when people discussed a case and production never changed. Tabletop exercises are the wrong label when the team actually moved users or injected a live alert. Tabletop exercises start from a written scenario with timed injects that force a decision, not from an open chat. Tabletop exercises ask who calls customers, who may spend money, and who may shut a service down. Tabletop exercises look for the downtime packet during the hour and note if nobody can find it. Tabletop exercises include a deputy for every critical role because the usual expert may be away. Tabletop exercises end with three fixes at most so the list is short enough to finish. Tabletop exercises schedule the next session before people leave, aimed at the largest gap. Tabletop exercises keep production credentials out of the room so the talk cannot become an accidental change. Tabletop exercises capture whether the call tree uses a path that still works when mail is down.',
        'r0': 'Tabletop exercises facilitate a scenario discussion that exposes authority, communications, and procedure gaps without touching production. Tabletop exercises cannot validate replica boot, restore integrity, or detection tooling. Tabletop exercises sit beside failover tests and simulation tests as a different kind of evidence. Tabletop exercises assign each gap an owner and a retest, and include deputies rather than only the usual expert. Tabletop exercises are the right label for a walkthrough and the wrong label for a live cutover or an injected alert. Tabletop exercises use timed injects, a facilitator, and a short fix list with owners. Tabletop exercises include deputies and a voice path that still rings when electronic mail is down. Tabletop exercises store the notes where the next facilitator can start without reconstructing folklore.',
        'r1': 'Tabletop exercises talk through an incident. Tabletop exercises do not break production. Tabletop exercises find missing decisions and missing people. Tabletop exercises cannot prove a restore works. Tabletop exercises are not an actual failover. Tabletop exercises are not an injected alert. Tabletop exercises need the real roles in the room. Tabletop exercises end with owners and dates. Tabletop exercises should include a deputy. Tabletop exercises are discussion evidence only. Tabletop exercises use timed injects and a facilitator. Tabletop exercises end with a short list of fixes. Tabletop exercises include a deputy for each key role.',
        'r2': 'Tabletop exercises work like reading a play aloud in a rehearsal room, with no audience and no scenery to break. Tabletop exercises show who does not know the next line, which is the point of the reading. Tabletop exercises cannot prove the set will stand, because nobody built the set. Tabletop exercises are not opening night in the spare theater. Tabletop exercises are not a smoke cue fired into the real auditorium. Tabletop exercises end when the cast lists the missing lines and who will write them before the next reading. Tabletop exercises stop the reading when the missing lines have names next to them and a date to return. Tabletop exercises invite the understudy, because the lead will be gone on the night that matters.',
        'r4': 'Schedule Tabletop exercises with the decision makers and their deputies. Keep Tabletop exercises off production systems. Record Tabletop exercises gaps with an owner and a date before the room empties. Retest Tabletop exercises fixes in a later session. Separate Tabletop exercises notes from failover timings and from alert-response timings. Reject Tabletop exercises as proof that a backup restores. Invite Tabletop exercises deputies, not only the usual expert. Limit Tabletop exercises follow-ups to a short list with dates.',
        'exam': 'Tabletop exercises is the label when people talk through a scenario and production stays untouched. Tabletop exercises is not the label when the stem says the team moved the workload or injected an alert. Tabletop exercises finds authority and procedure gaps, and does not prove a restore. Tabletop exercises expects roles, a scenario, and written follow-ups as the method.',
    },
    'Failover tests': {
        'body': 'A failover test actually moves the work to the spare and shows that users can do the job there. You time how long the application is quiet. A conversation about moving, however careful, is not this test. The spare has to carry real work while you watch.\n\nThe move is planned, announced, and reversible. You pick a window, fail the chosen system, and have a named group perform a real task on the spare. You record the start, the moment the task succeeds, and any step that stalled. Production may be disrupted on purpose, which is why the window is agreed. Other exercises answer other questions. A tabletop talks through a story and does not touch the running system. A simulation may inject signals to see whether detection and response work. A parallel test runs the spare beside production and compares results while the original system stays in charge. Those are useful. None of them is a failover until the work itself has moved.\n\nOn Sunday at 02:00 the database was failed to the second node. The clinic application was quiet for 4 minutes and 12 seconds. Two test users then completed a chart lookup on the spare and saved the timestamps. Ticket DR-FO-22 holds the quiet interval, the node that took over, and the lookup result. The team did not merely read the runbook aloud. They also did not leave production untouched while a copy ran beside it. The lookup is the proof that users could work. A green cluster icon without that lookup would have been a weaker claim.\n\nDefenders schedule the test often enough that the runbook cannot rot, and they include a user task rather than only a ping. Compare the quiet time with the recovery target. If the target is 15 minutes and the quiet period is 4 minutes, record the pass and still note any manual step that would fail at noon on a weekday. Fix the stall before the next test. Do not skip the failback. Returning to the original node is part of knowing the design works in both directions. Invite the person who would declare a real event, not only the person who types the command.\n\nStudents call a tabletop a failover test because both are drills. Talking is not moving. Students call a side-by-side comparison a failover when production never left the original system. The separating fact is the move. If users did the job on the spare and you can say how long the job was quiet, you have a failover test. If you only discussed it, you have a tabletop.\n',
        'r0': 'A failover test moves the work and times it. DR-FO-22 failed the database to the second node on Sunday at 02:00. The application was quiet for 4 minutes and 12 seconds, then test users finished a chart lookup on the spare. A tabletop only discusses the story. A simulation can inject signals and still leave production in place. A parallel run compares a spare beside production and does not take production away. Compare the quiet interval with the recovery target. Practice failback as well as the move. Evidence is the timed quiet period plus a user task that succeeded on the spare.\n',
        'r1': 'Actually move the work to the spare. Time how long the usual system is quiet. Have a person complete a real task there. Talking through a story is not this test. Running a copy beside the live system is not this test either. A practice alarm can be useful and still not move the job. Write the start time and the finish time. Practice returning to the original system too. If nobody used the spare, you did not prove the move. Compare the quiet time with the promise you made.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture the clinic actually serving Saturday lunch once in the spare kitchen, with a clock on the pass. You move the orders, you plate a real meal, and you write down the minutes the dining room waited. A meeting about that lunch, held on Thursday, never heats the spare stove. Cooking the same soup in both kitchens and tasting both, while the main kitchen still serves, answers a different question. In the database drill, the Sunday move is that Saturday service. The chart lookup is the plated meal, and the 4 quiet minutes are the time the dining room waited.\n',
        'r4': 'Open DR-FO-22 and record the Sunday 02:00 start. Record the 4 minutes and 12 seconds of quiet time. Attach the chart lookup completed on the spare. Do not relabel a tabletop discussion as this test. Do not relabel a side-by-side comparison as this test. Note whether failback was practiced. Stop when the ticket shows that real work moved and how long users waited.\n',
        'exam': 'Actually moving the work is a failover test. A tabletop conversation is not. A side-by-side comparison that leaves production in place is not. Injected alerts without a cutover are not. The quiet time belongs in the answer. A user task on the spare belongs in the answer. Failback is part of a complete test. The stem to recognize is users working on the spare after a real switch.\n',
    },
    'Simulation tests': {
        'body': 'Simulation tests stage a lifelike case, sometimes by inserting a signal, and watch whether noticing and responding actually happen. Simulation tests time the path from the first signal to the right human, and from that human to a useful action. Simulation tests may include a planned phishing exercise that measures reporting, not a surprise aimed at humiliating staff. Simulation tests may include a synthetic alert in the monitoring system that should page on-call. Simulation tests differ from tabletop exercises, which do not inject a signal and do not touch detection tools. Simulation tests differ from failover tests, which move the service to a spare rather than testing whether anyone notices. Simulation tests should be announced to the people who must not be tricked, such as executives who would treat a drill as real. Simulation tests should still be realistic for the responder, or the timing is meaningless. Simulation tests record false assumptions, such as a page that went to a mailbox nobody reads. Simulation tests record whether the runbook step matched the product the team actually has. Simulation tests keep production safe: an injected alert must not delete real data or isolate a real plant network. Simulation tests coordinate with anyone who might escalate to law enforcement or to customers. Simulation tests use a unique marker in the inject so analysts can separate drill events from true events. Simulation tests review the result with the people who responded, while the minutes are still fresh. Simulation tests assign fixes for a missed page, a broken contact, or a playbook step that pointed at the wrong tool. Simulation tests repeat after on-call membership changes. Simulation tests do not confuse this drill with a vulnerability scan or a full red-team engagement. Simulation tests do not claim it proves backups restore or that a hot site can take users. Simulation tests can sit on a range or on production-like telemetry if the blast radius is controlled. Simulation tests include the handoff between shifts if the scenario is long. Simulation tests include the decision to escalate severity, because many drills fail at that sentence. Simulation tests store timelines in the same place as the incident notes so the format is familiar. Simulation tests fix detection first when the injected signal never fired. Simulation tests fix the page path and the ownership when the signal fired and nobody moved. Simulation tests match the ticket when the crew rehearsed noticing and responding under a lifelike case. Simulation tests are the wrong label when the only activity was a conversation or a cutover. Simulation tests respect people during a phishing-style drill and measure the reporting path you want them to use. Simulation tests tell participants what was practiced after the window, and what will change. Simulation tests avoid injecting into safety systems where a fake signal could move equipment. Simulation tests keep the scenario aligned to a duty you actually have. Simulation tests agree the success action in advance, such as a ticket opened or a page acknowledged. Simulation tests separate drill telemetry with a marker the daily report can filter. Simulation tests debrief the responders before the end of the shift that ran the drill. Simulation tests track the escalation sentence, because many rehearsals fail when severity must rise. Simulation tests keep a rollback for any inject that touched a shared channel.',
        'r0': 'Simulation tests inject a realistic signal, such as a synthetic page or a controlled reporting exercise, and time the human response. Simulation tests keep a marker so drill events separate from real incidents, and keep a blast radius that cannot delete live data. Simulation tests differ from tabletops that never touch detection tools and from failovers that move the workload. Simulation tests measure time to the right person and time to a useful action, then fix missed pages before adding paperwork. Simulation tests are the wrong label for a pure discussion or for a site cutover. Simulation tests exclude safety-system injects that could move real equipment. Simulation tests define the success action, filter drill telemetry with a marker, and debrief the same shift.',
        'r1': 'Simulation tests rehearse detection and response. Simulation tests may inject a signal or a page. Simulation tests time how long until a person acts. Simulation tests are not a tabletop discussion. Simulation tests are not a site cutover. Simulation tests must not destroy real data. Simulation tests need a marker that shows it is a drill. Simulation tests fix a missed page before adding paper. Simulation tests do not prove a backup restore. Simulation tests should teach the reporting path you want.',
        'r2': 'Simulation tests work like a lifeboat drill that uses a painted flare so the crew must still muster. Simulation tests time the muster from the painted flare to the right boat, not from a conversation in the galley. Simulation tests are not a script reading, and they are not sailing the spare ship out of the harbor. Simulation tests must not start a real fire in the hold just to make the drill vivid. Simulation tests show whether the bell rings and whether anyone walks. Simulation tests end when the crew writes down why a bell failed and who will repair it.',
        'r4': 'Mark Simulation tests injects so analysts can separate them from real events. Time Simulation tests from the first signal to a useful human action. Keep Simulation tests away from safety systems and from destructive steps. Review Simulation tests with responders before the details fade. Fix Simulation tests paging gaps before you add more pages to the binder. Separate Simulation tests records from failover timings and from discussion notes.',
        'exam': 'Simulation tests is the label when a realistic scenario or an injected signal tests detection and response. Simulation tests is not the label for a talk-only exercise or for an actual workload move. Simulation tests expects a timed human response and a controlled blast radius. Simulation tests does not, by itself, prove that backups restore or that a spare site can serve users.',
    },
    'Parallel processing tests': {
        'body': "Parallel processing tests run the spare beside production and compare results before anyone trusts the spare with real users. Parallel processing tests keep the primary in service the whole time, which is why this test does not measure a user-visible cutover. Parallel processing tests feed the spare a copy of the day's transactions, orders, or lab results and compare totals. Parallel processing tests treat a mismatch as a defect in the spare, not as a rounding story to ignore. Parallel processing tests differ from failover tests, which actually move users and time the quiet period. Parallel processing tests differ from tabletop exercises, which never run the spare at all. Parallel processing tests need a comparison method written in advance: which fields, which tolerance, which time window. Parallel processing tests need enough realistic volume that a toy sample cannot hide a broken rule. Parallel processing tests isolate the spare's outputs so a test write cannot leak into customer-facing systems. Parallel processing tests watch cost and load, because running both at full volume can saturate links or double license use. Parallel processing tests document how fresh the copy was, since a stale feed makes a false mismatch. Parallel processing tests document code and configuration versions on both sides so a mismatch has a suspect. Parallel processing tests include a person from the business who can say whether the totals mean the same thing. Parallel processing tests do not declare the spare ready for failover on this evidence alone. Parallel processing tests do use this evidence before a first failover of a system that posts money or results. Parallel processing tests record the match rate and the few exceptions by type. Parallel processing tests fix exceptions and rerun, rather than waiving them in a footnote. Parallel processing tests schedule the run when production has a representative mix, not on a holiday. Parallel processing tests protect personal data in the copied feed the same way production is protected. Parallel processing tests expire the copied feed when the test ends. Parallel processing tests separate this label from backup restores, which rebuild from a copy rather than comparing a live twin. Parallel processing tests separate this label from load balancing, which spreads real requests rather than shadowing them. Parallel processing tests can reveal a slow query on the spare that a boot test would never show. Parallel processing tests can reveal a missing integration that only appears when a full day is processed. Parallel processing tests still need a later failover test after a passing comparison, to learn the quiet-period number. Parallel processing tests must block the cutover after a failed comparison. Parallel processing tests match the ticket when both sides ran and a person compared the outputs. Parallel processing tests are the wrong label when the team only talked or only moved production. Parallel processing tests keep the comparison artifacts with the change record. Parallel processing tests name the owner who may say the totals are close enough. Parallel processing tests name the fields, the tolerance, and the time window before either side runs. Parallel processing tests record code versions on both sides so a mismatch has a suspect to investigate. Parallel processing tests expire the copied feed when the comparison ends and keep it as protected as production. Parallel processing tests include a business reader who can say whether two totals mean the same thing. Parallel processing tests stop any cutover plan while exceptions are still unexplained.",
        'r0': 'Parallel processing tests shadow production on the spare, compare agreed totals, and keep spare writes off the live record. Parallel processing tests do not measure cutover time, so they complement failover tests rather than replacing them. Parallel processing tests require a pre-written comparison, representative volume, and version notes for both sides. Parallel processing tests stay distinct from discussion exercises and from a restore drill. Parallel processing tests treat mismatches as defects and rerun after fixes before a first cutover of a high-stakes system. Parallel processing tests freeze the comparison rules, the versions, and the tolerance before the run starts. Parallel processing tests expire the copied feed afterward and block cutover while exceptions remain.',
        'r1': 'Parallel processing tests run the spare beside production. Parallel processing tests compare results before you trust it. Parallel processing tests do not move users. Parallel processing tests are not a failover timing. Parallel processing tests are not a tabletop talk. Parallel processing tests need a comparison method first. Parallel processing tests need realistic volume. Parallel processing tests must not write into the live record. Parallel processing tests do not replace a later cutover test. Parallel processing tests stop the cutover plan if totals disagree.',
        'r2': 'Parallel processing tests work like two translators rendering the same page while the original speaker keeps talking. Parallel processing tests taste both pages before you dismiss the first translator. Parallel processing tests do not ask the audience to switch rooms during the tasting. Parallel processing tests are not a table reading of the play, and they are not sending the crowd into another hall. Parallel processing tests fail if the second translator had only the first sentence and you still called it a full page. Parallel processing tests end when the pages match, or when you refuse to switch until they do.',
        'r4': 'Compare Parallel processing tests outputs with a method written before the run. Keep Parallel processing tests writes off live customer records. Use Parallel processing tests volume that looks like a real day. Rerun Parallel processing tests after you fix a mismatch. Refuse Parallel processing tests as a substitute for a timed cutover. File Parallel processing tests totals with the change record.',
        'exam': 'Parallel processing tests is the label when the spare processes a live copy beside production and the results are compared. Parallel processing tests should be refused when the stem is only a discussion, a user cutover, or a restore onto a blank host. Parallel processing tests does not time user-visible downtime, because production never left. Parallel processing tests should block a first cutover when the totals do not match.',
    },
    'Onsite and offsite backups': {
        'body': 'Onsite backups sit near the systems they protect, so a restore is fast and a building fire can take the copy with the original. Offsite backups sit far enough away to survive that fire, and they take longer to reach. You usually want both, for different failures. Neither one is a mirror of the live disks.\n\nA copy in the server-room closet can return a deleted file before the appointment ends. It cannot survive the fire, the flood, or the theft that takes the room. A copy in another region survives those events and may take hours to retrieve, depending on the network or the courier. The schedule should say which copy answers which kind of loss. A copy on another volume in the same server is not offsite. A copy in a closet down the hall is still onsite. Offsite means a different site. A disk mirror or RAID set keeps the system up when one drive fails, and it deletes, encrypts, or burns with the original. RAID is not a backup. If the only copy is the mirror, you do not have a backup location of either kind.\n\nThe clinic writes a nightly copy to a closet unit in the server room and a second copy to object storage in another region. Ticket BK-14 records a deleted scan restored from the closet in about 20 minutes, and it records the regional copy as the one that would remain after a room fire. The closet unit uses RAID so a single disk failure does not eat the onsite copy. That RAID set is still one onsite backup, not a second site, and not a substitute for the regional copy. When someone proposed dropping the regional copy because the closet unit was redundant inside itself, the ticket rejected the proposal. Redundant disks are not an offsite backup.\n\nDefenders name the restore you are buying with each location. Test both: a file from the closet, and a file from the far copy, on a schedule. Time them. Confirm the far copy is not in the same building or the same flood plain as the closet. Encrypt the media or the bucket, and keep that key somewhere the fire does not include. Review requests to keep only the fast copy. Speed is real. It is the wrong priority for the disaster that destroys the building.\n\nStudents call the closet copy offsite because it is not the original server. Near is still onsite. Students call a RAID mirror a backup location. It is resilience for a failed disk, and it is not a restorable copy from before a delete. The separating fact is fate and speed. The near copy is fast and shares the building. The far copy is slower and survives the building. You can state both without pretending they are one thing.\n',
        'r0': "Onsite backups restore quickly and share the building's fate. Offsite backups survive the building and take longer to reach. BK-14 restored a deleted scan from the server-room closet in about 20 minutes, and a second copy lives in another region's object storage. A volume on the same server is not offsite. A closet down the hall is still onsite. RAID on the closet unit survives a disk failure and is not a backup. A mirror deletes and encrypts with the original. Test a restore from each location. Keep the far copy outside the same flood plain. Evidence is two copies with two different fates, each one actually restored.\n",
        'r1': 'A nearby backup is fast and dies in the same fire. A far backup survives the building and takes longer to fetch. You often want one of each. A copy in the same room is not a far copy. A copy down the hall is still nearby. A disk mirror is not a backup. The mirror loses the file when you delete it. The mirror burns when the room burns. Practice a restore from both places. Keep the key for the far copy outside the fire too.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a recipe box in the clinic kitchen drawer and another box in a lockbox at a building across town. The drawer box is in your hand in a minute, and a kitchen fire takes it with the stove. The lockbox survives that fire, and you spend the afternoon going to get it. A second card taped to the same drawer is not the lockbox. A mirrored shelf that always matches the counter loses a recipe the moment someone scraps the original. In the backup plan, the server-room closet is the drawer and the other region is the lockbox. The disk mirror is the shelf that cannot remember yesterday once today is ruined.\n',
        'r4': 'On BK-14, record the 20-minute closet restore and the region that holds the far copy. Label the closet copy onsite and the regional copy offsite. Do not call a same-building closet offsite. Do not call the RAID set a separate backup. Confirm a test restore exists for each location. Note where the encryption key for the far copy lives. Stop when the ticket gives each copy a fate and a tested restore.\n',
        'exam': 'Near is fast and shares the fire. Far survives the fire and is slower. A closet in the same building is onsite. RAID is not a backup. A mirror does not save you from deletion or from ransomware. Dropping the far copy because disks are redundant leaves you with one fate. The stem to recognize is a pair of copies kept for two different kinds of loss.\n',
    },
    'Backup frequency and encryption': {
        'body': "Frequency decides how much recent work you can lose. Encryption decides whether a stolen backup can be read. They are separate knobs on the same copy. A nightly encrypted tape can be useless to a thief and still leave you missing everything typed since last night.\n\nHow often you capture sets the recovery point. A full backup copies the selected data. An incremental backup copies only what changed since the last backup of any kind, full or incremental. A differential backup copies what changed since the last full backup. Incrementals stay small and must be restored in order after the full. Differentials grow until the next full, and a restore needs the full plus the latest differential only. Students swap those two starting points, and the swap breaks the restore. RAID is not any of these. A mirror is not a full, not an incremental, and not a differential. Encryption, often AES-256 for the backup file or the tape, locks the copy. The key must live somewhere other than inside the same stolen bag. Encryption does not change how much work a lunchtime mistake can lose.\n\nThe clinic file server takes a full backup on Sunday at 01:00 and an incremental each weeknight at 01:00. Thursday's incremental contains changes since Wednesday's incremental, not since Sunday. A Thursday restore needs Sunday's full plus Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, and Thursday incrementals, in order. If those weeknight jobs were differentials, Thursday's job would hold every change since Sunday, and the restore would be Sunday plus Thursday only. Ticket BK-ENC-8 records both the schedule and the AES-256 key stored on the vault appliance, not on the tape. A file deleted Wednesday at noon is gone until restore, so the loss window is about a day. A stolen Tuesday tape without the vault key does not yield the files.\n\nDefenders write the recovery point next to the schedule so leadership sees the lunchtime gap. Test a restore that uses the full and each later incremental, because a missing tape in the chain fails the incremental design. Do not store the only key on the media you might lose. When someone proposes differentials, update the restore steps on purpose rather than mixing the two chains. Revisit frequency when the clinic cannot afford a day of lost scans. Faster incrementals shrink that window and add jobs you must monitor.\n\nStudents define an incremental as changes since the last full backup. That definition is the differential. Students define a differential as changes since yesterday's incremental. That definition is the incremental. The separating fact is the starting point of the copy. Any prior backup means incremental. The last full backup means differential. Encryption is the other half of the stem: a readable stolen tape is a frequency plan that forgot the lock.\n",
        'r0': "Frequency sets the recovery point. Encryption sets whether a stolen copy is readable. A full backup copies the selection. An incremental copies changes since the last backup of any kind. A differential copies changes since the last full backup. Sunday 01:00 full plus weeknight incrementals means a Thursday restore needs Sunday and every incremental through Thursday, in order. BK-ENC-8 locks those copies with AES-256, and the key stays on the vault appliance, not on the tape. A Wednesday noon delete can wait until the next night's job. RAID is not a full, an incremental, or a differential. Test the whole incremental chain. Evidence is the schedule, the restore order, and the key location.\n",
        'r1': "How often you copy decides how much new work you can lose. A lock on the copy decides whether a thief can read it. A full copy takes the whole set. An incremental takes changes since the last copy of any kind. A differential takes changes since the last full copy. Incrementals must be put back in order. A differential restore uses the full set plus the newest differential. A disk mirror is not one of these copies. Do not leave the only key on the stolen tape. A daily job can still lose a morning's work.\n",
        'r2': "Picture a clinic kitchen that copies its order book. Sunday's book is the full stack. Each night's incremental is only the new slips since the previous night, whatever kind of copy that night was. A differential night would instead recopy every slip since Sunday's stack, growing thicker all week. You rebuild an incremental week by laying Sunday down and then every thin night in order. You rebuild a differential week with Sunday plus the latest thick night. A lockbox around the slips stops a thief from reading them and does not fill in the slips you never copied after lunch. On the file server, that book is the backup set, and the vault key is the lockbox combination that must not ride inside the box.\n",
        'r4': 'Write the Sunday full and the weeknight incremental times from BK-ENC-8. State that each incremental starts at the previous backup of any kind. Do not describe that job as changes since Sunday unless you are documenting a differential. Record that AES-256 keys stay on the vault appliance. Note the Wednesday noon delete as a loss until the next job. Do not call a disk mirror any of these backups. Stop when the restore order and the key location are both on the ticket.\n',
        'exam': 'An incremental copies changes since the last backup of any kind. A differential copies changes since the last full backup. Swapping those starting points is the common miss. Incremental restore needs every link in order. Differential restore needs the full backup plus the latest differential. Encryption does not shrink the hours you can lose. RAID is not a backup of either kind. The stem to recognize is how far back the copy reaches, plus whether a stolen copy can be read.\n',
    },
    'Snapshots, replication, and journals': {
        'body': "A snapshot, a replica, and a journal are three different tools that people file under the word backup. A snapshot is a point-in-time image. Replication copies changes to another system as they happen or on a short delay. A journal records the changes so you can roll forward after you restore an older image. Using the wrong name picks the wrong restore.\n\nA snapshot often uses copy-on-write storage on the same array as the live volume. It is fast to take and fast to revert, and it dies with that array. It is a weak place to hide from a fire or from a storage failure. Replication sends updates to another host, so the second host can be minutes behind and can sit in another room or another region. Replication also sends the bad news: a mass delete or an encryption attack is copied if you do not pause it. A journal, such as a database transaction log, lists committed changes. After you restore a snapshot or a full backup, you replay the journal to a chosen minute. The journal is not the image, and it is not the second host. RAID is none of the three. A mirror keeps the live set online when a disk fails, and it is not a point in time you can roll back to after a delete.\n\nTicket DB-33 describes the payroll database. Snapshot snap-0314 was taken at 12:00 on the same array as the live volume. Replica db-replica-02 stayed about three minutes behind on a different host. After a bad load at 14:00, the team restored the snapshot and replayed the journal through 13:50, stopping before the bad load. The replica had already received part of the bad load, so failing over to it would have kept the damage. The journal was what made 13:50 possible. The snapshot alone would have stopped at noon and lost almost two hours of good work.\n\nDefenders keep at least one snapshot or backup that replication will not overwrite, and they keep journals long enough to cover the gap you care about. Put a replica in a different failure domain if the replica is your survival copy, and still do not assume it is clean. Test a roll-forward to a minute you choose, not only a revert to the snapshot. Monitor replica lag. A replica that is hours behind is a different recovery point from the one you briefed. Do not delete journals to save disk without agreeing the minutes you can no longer reach.\n\nStudents call every copy a snapshot. A second system receiving a stream is replication. A list of transactions you can replay is a journal. Students call a snapshot on the same array an offsite backup. It shares the array's fate. The separating fact is what you are holding. An image at one moment, a live second system, or a log you can roll forward are three answers, and the ticket should say which one was used.\n",
        'r0': 'A snapshot is a point-in-time image. Replication copies changes to another system. A journal records changes so you can roll forward. DB-33 took snap-0314 at 12:00 on the same array as the live volume. Replica db-replica-02 stayed about three minutes behind and had already taken part of the 14:00 bad load. Restoring the snapshot and replaying the journal to 13:50 avoided that load. Failing over to the replica would have kept it. RAID is not a snapshot, a replica, or a journal. Same-array snapshots die with the array. Evidence is which of the three tools produced the minute you recovered.\n',
        'r1': 'A snapshot is a picture of one moment. A replica is another system receiving changes. A journal is a list of changes you can replay. The picture is often stored with the original disks. The picture can die with those disks. The replica can copy a mistake as well as good work. The journal lets you move forward to a chosen minute. A disk mirror is none of these. Do not fail over onto a replica that already copied the damage. Say which tool gave you the minute you kept.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a pantry photo, a second pantry that clerks update all afternoon, and the paper list of items added since the photo. The photo is quick to look at and useless if the whole shelf unit is hauled away. The second pantry stays nearly current and also receives a spoiled delivery if nobody stops the clerks. The paper list lets you restock from the photo and then add only the good items up to a chosen hour. A mirrored shelf that always matches the counter cannot show noon once the counter is wrong. In the database story, the noon image is the photo, the other host is the second pantry, and the replay to 13:50 is the paper list stopping before the spoiled delivery.\n',
        'r4': 'On DB-33, record snap-0314 at 12:00 and where that image lives. Record that db-replica-02 was about three minutes behind and had taken bad changes. Record the journal replay that stopped at 13:50. Do not call the replica a journal. Do not call the same-array image an offsite copy. Do not call a disk mirror any of the three. Stop when the ticket names the image, the second system, and the replay separately.\n',
        'exam': 'A snapshot is a point-in-time image. Replication is a second system receiving changes. A journal is the log you roll forward. A same-array snapshot is not an offsite backup. A replica can copy corruption. RAID is not a backup and not these three tools. The stem to recognize is which object you have: a photo, a live copy, or a list of changes.\n',
    },
    'Recovery testing': {
        'body': "Recovery testing proves a copy can come back, because a backup nobody has restored is only a hope. Recovery testing restores onto an isolated host so the test cannot overwrite production. Recovery testing opens a real file, runs a real query, or signs into a real application before calling it success. Recovery testing does this on a schedule, such as a quarterly sample of a different system each time. Recovery testing times the restore and compares it with the recovery time objective. Recovery testing checks the recovered point against the recovery point objective. Recovery testing includes the key ceremony, the offsite retrieval, and the permissions, not only the happy path on local disk. Recovery testing records who performed it and which backup set they used. Recovery testing files the gap when the set is incomplete, the key is missing, or the application will not start. Recovery testing feeds that gap to the backup owner the same week. Recovery testing differs from a snapshot existing, a replica being online, or a generator starting. Recovery testing differs from a failover of a live replica, which may never touch the backup sets. Recovery testing should occasionally restore a full system, not only one small file, so missing system state is visible. Recovery testing should sample databases, file stores, and identity data across the year. Recovery testing should confirm the restored data is the sensitive data, not an empty shell with the right name. Recovery testing keeps the isolated host fenced so test data cannot become a new leak. Recovery testing removes the test host when the check is done, or it becomes an unpatched copy of production. Recovery testing updates the runbook with the step that surprised the tester. Recovery testing does not accept a vendor's green dashboard as a substitute for opening the data. Recovery testing does not skip a year because the last test passed. Recovery testing after ransomware planning, include a test from the immutable or offline generation. Recovery testing match the ticket when someone restored a set and opened the data to check it. Recovery testing sample a different system each quarter so file stores, databases, and identity each get a turn. Recovery testing include the offline or immutable generation, not only the copy that is easiest to reach. Recovery testing write the backup-set name, the tester, and the elapsed minutes in the record. Recovery testing fence the test host and remove it when the check ends so it does not linger unpatched. Recovery testing treat a green job dashboard as incomplete until someone opens the restored data. Recovery testing confirm permissions and ownership came back, not only the bytes.",
        'r0': 'Recovery testing restores a real set to an isolated host, opens real data, and times the work against the recovery time objective. Recovery testing includes offsite retrieval and key access, then destroys or fences the test copy. Recovery testing samples different systems across the year, including an immutable generation. Recovery testing is not a replica failover, a snapshot listing, or a generator run. Recovery testing turns gaps into runbook changes the same week. Recovery testing record the set name, the tester, and the minutes, including key retrieval. Recovery testing confirm permissions came back and then remove the fenced test host. Recovery testing sample an immutable generation, not only the newest online copy.',
        'r1': 'Recovery testing proves a copy can come back. Recovery testing uses an isolated host. Recovery testing opens real data before success. Recovery testing times the work against the downtime cap. Recovery testing includes keys and offsite retrieval. Recovery testing is not a replica failover. Recovery testing is not a power test. Recovery testing must happen more than once. Recovery testing must sample more than one easy file. Recovery testing feeds gaps back into the runbook. Recovery testing sample a different system each quarter. Recovery testing include the copy that is hardest to reach.',
        'r2': 'Recovery testing works like playing a spare recording all the way through to be sure the song is really on the tape. Recovery testing does not count a labeled box as music if nobody has listened. Recovery testing uses a spare room so you do not tape over the master. Recovery testing is not starting the spare band on stage, and they are not turning on the building generator. Recovery testing includes finding the key to the cabinet and walking to the other building if that is where the tape lives. Recovery testing ends when you hear the song, or when you admit the tape is blank.',
        'r4': 'Restore Recovery testing samples onto an isolated host every quarter. Open Recovery testing data with a real query or a real file before you pass it. Time Recovery testing against the downtime promise, including key retrieval. Include Recovery testing from an offline or immutable generation. Remove Recovery testing hosts when the check is finished. Rewrite Recovery testing runbooks with the step that failed.',
        'exam': 'Recovery testing is the label when someone restores a copy and opens real data on an isolated host. Recovery testing is not the label for an untested backup job, a live replica switch, or a power exercise. Recovery testing expects a clock, a key path, and a sample that is more than a green dashboard. Recovery testing treats an untested copy as hope, not as a backup you can claim.',
    },
    'Generators': {
        'body': 'Generators make electrical power after the utility fails, following a short start delay, for as long as the fuel lasts. Generators need fuel, a maintained engine, a transfer switch, and a load test. Generators differ from uninterruptible power supplies, which bridge seconds or minutes with batteries and do not run all night. Generators should be sized for the real load, including cooling, not for a single rack on a hopeful spreadsheet. Generators should be tested under load on a schedule, because a no-load start hides a weak alternator. Generators should record runtime at the tested load so the fuel tank has a meaning in hours. Generators need a fuel contract and a way to refuel if the outage lasts longer than the tank. Generators need exhaust, clearance, and a location that is not the first place a flood reaches. Generators start after the battery bridge, so the design is battery first and generator second. Generators will not save open files if the battery was too small and systems died during the start delay. Generators belong in the recovery time story for the building, not in the data-restore story. Generators do not replace backups, replicas, or a second site. Generators must be included in failover planning if the spare room is in the same building and the utility is the hazard. Generators may be irrelevant to a regional outage if the spare site is elsewhere and already powered. Generators need someone who may start them manually if the automatic transfer fails. Generators need a runbook the facilities person and the technology person have both read. Generators should not be blocked by a starter battery that nobody has replaced. Generators should alarm before fuel is below the runtime you promised. Generators after each test, note whether the server room stayed up and whether cooling stayed up. Generators match the ticket when the facts show on-site power making, fuel, and a start delay. Generators stay off the ticket for a battery that only holds a brief interval. Generators include the starter battery and the transfer switch in the same test as the engine. Generators note whether cooling stayed up, because compute without cooling is a short victory. Generators name the person who may start the set manually if the automatic transfer fails. Generators alarm while fuel can still cover the promised hours, not after the tank is empty. Generators keep the runbook shared with facilities and with the technology on-call. Generators record the tested load in watts so the next sizing talk has a number from life.',
        'r0': "Generators supply power after a start delay for a fuel-limited runtime, and they require load tests and refueling plans. Generators depend on an uninterruptible supply to cover the start gap and the clean-shutdown case. Generators size cooling and compute together, and alarm on fuel before the promised runtime disappears. Generators are not batteries, backups, or a remote site. Generators fail the evidence test when the device only bridges a blink and has no fuel. Generators test the transfer switch, the starter battery, and cooling under load, not only a no-load start. Generators name a manual-start owner and alarm on fuel while the promised hours still remain. Generators record tested watts so the tank's hours are measured rather than hoped.",
        'r1': 'Generators make power when the utility is out. Generators start after a short delay. Generators need fuel and a load test. Generators need a way to refuel. Generators are not a battery bridge. Generators are not a backup copy. Generators are not a second site. Generators must include cooling load. Generators must alarm on low fuel. Generators fail if nobody has tested them under load. Generators include the transfer switch in the test. Generators name who may start them manually. Generators record tested watts, not a guess.',
        'r2': 'Generators work like a yard pump that can pull water after the city main fails, once the motor catches. Generators need fuel in the tank, or the pump is a sculpture. Generators need a moment to catch, so a small cistern must cover that moment. Generators are not the cistern, and they are not a second well in another town. Generators are not a spare copy of the account book. Generators prove themselves when someone runs the pump against a real load and times the tank. Generators include the hose, the starter, and the person allowed to pull the cord, not only the motor. Generators time the tank while water is actually moving, and write that duration where the crew can see it.',
        'r4': 'Test Generators under real load and record the runtime the tank actually supports. Fuel Generators under a contract that works on a holiday. Pair Generators with a battery bridge that covers the start delay. Alarm Generators before fuel falls below the promised hours. Include Generators cooling load in the size, not only the server nameplates. Reject Generators as the label for a battery that lasts minutes.',
        'exam': 'Generators is the label when the device makes power after a delay and needs fuel and a load test. Generators is not the label for a battery bridge of seconds or minutes. Generators is not a backup and not a spare site. Generators fails the stem when it has never been run under load or the tank has no refuel plan.',
    },
    'Uninterruptible power supplies': {
        'body': "An uninterruptible power supply is a battery bridge for the racks. It carries the load through a utility blink, or long enough for a generator to start, or long enough for the systems to shut down cleanly. It is not an overnight power plant. The useful number is the runtime at the load you actually draw, which for the clinic racks is about five minutes.\n\nThe battery sits in the power path, often through an inverter that keeps feeding the equipment when the wall feed drops. An online unit is already supplying the racks from that conditioned path, so a short dip never reaches the server power supplies. There is no wait for an engine to catch. Minutes later the battery is done unless something else has taken over. A generator makes power for hours after a transfer delay, and it needs fuel plus a start test. It does not cover the first seconds. A surge strip clamps spikes and stores nothing, so it cannot hold a server up during an outage. Runtime shrinks as you add equipment. The nameplate rating is a guess until you measure the current draw.\n\nTicket FAC-UPS-2 records a 12-second utility blink at 03:40. The five-minute unit held the server racks, and the payroll database did not restart. Utility returned before a generator was required. The same unit would not have carried those racks until morning, and it was never written up as if it could. Facilities later measured closer to four minutes at the present load, because two hosts had been added since the purchase. That measured runtime, not the brochure, is what the facilities note should quote.\n\nDefenders test the transfer onto battery, replace batteries when the self-test fails, and alarm both the on-battery state and a failed battery. Recalculate runtime when a rack grows. Confirm the shutdown software can park the databases if the generator does not start. Keep the measured minutes in the same document as any recovery timeline so nobody schedules a two-hour restore on a five-minute bridge. Do not describe the generator's fuel tank as if it were inside the UPS.\n\nStudents use one name for every backup-power object. The generator is the engine that starts after a delay and then runs for a long time if it has fuel. This device is the short bridge in front of that delay. A surge strip is neither. The separating fact is the source and the duration. Seconds or a few minutes of battery, as in the 12-second blink on FAC-UPS-2, means the UPS. Fuel and hours of runtime means the generator.\n",
        'r0': 'An uninterruptible power supply bridges seconds or minutes until a generator starts or the systems shut down cleanly. FAC-UPS-2 held the racks through a 12-second blink at 03:40 on a unit planned for about five minutes. Measured runtime later fell toward four minutes after two hosts were added. A generator covers hours after a transfer delay and needs fuel and a start test. A surge strip stores no energy. Online units feed the racks from the conditioned path before the outage. Alarm on-battery and failed self-tests. Do not plan a multi-hour restore on this bridge. Evidence is a short timed carry through a blink, plus a measured runtime at the real load.\n',
        'r1': 'This device is a short battery bridge. It holds equipment for seconds or a few minutes. It can wait while a generator starts. It can also give time for a clean shutdown. It is not an overnight power plan. A generator runs longer and needs fuel. A generator is not instant. A strip that only stops spikes is not a battery. Adding machines makes the battery last less time. Test the switch onto battery. Replace tired batteries on a schedule. Point at the short bridge when you name this device.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a battery lantern in a clinic stairwell during a brief power cut. The lantern is already lit, so the stairs stay visible for a few minutes while someone starts the building generator or walks people out. It is not a second power plant for the night. A generator in the yard can run for hours and still leaves the first moments dark if nothing bridges them. A cheap plug-in spike guard does not glow at all when the feed is gone. In the server room, the battery unit that carried the racks through the short blink is that lantern. The four or five measured minutes are how long the lantern actually lasts, not how long the yard generator could run.\n',
        'r4': 'Open FAC-UPS-2 and record the 12-second blink and the measured runtime. Quote the measured minutes, not only the purchase brochure. Do not describe this unit as overnight power. Keep the generator, its fuel, and its start delay in a separate note. Confirm a failed battery self-test would alarm. Confirm shutdown software can park the databases if the generator does not start. Stop when the ticket shows a short bridge and the load it was measured against.\n',
        'exam': 'The short battery bridge is this supply. A generator is the fueled engine that starts after a delay. A surge strip does not power an outage. Five minutes is not an overnight plan. Added load shortens runtime. A multi-hour restore cannot depend on the battery alone. The stem to recognize is seconds or a few minutes of carry while utility power blinks or a generator comes up.\n',
    },
    'Establishing a secure baseline': {
        'body': "Establishing a secure baseline means writing the approved settings once, from a known-good build, before any copies go out. The baseline is the signed description of that build: what is installed, what is turned off, and how the host should behave. Until someone owns that description, later copying has nothing trustworthy to copy.\n\nYou build one system carefully, measure it, and record the result. The record can include the image name, a hash of the image, the local policy, the firewall posture, and the software that is allowed to be present. A published benchmark can suggest settings. The clinic baseline is the build the endpoint owner signs, not a bookmark to someone else's document. Establishing stops at that sign-off. Putting the image on the next hundred laptops is the later act of deploying it. Finding a machine that drifted and pulling it back is maintenance. Mixing those three moments makes the ticket unclear about whether a standard even exists.\n\nLaptop image WIN11-CLINIC-2026.03 was built in the lab, measured, and signed by the endpoint owner on ticket BASE-EST-1 before any employee received a machine. The image hash is stored with the sign-off. The build turns off local administrator rights for ordinary users, enables disk encryption, and includes only the clinical software list. No one had been handed a laptop yet. A technician's request to click a one-off finance PC into shape the same week was refused, because that click path would have created a second, unsigned build. The refusal is part of establishing the baseline. The standard has to exist before the exceptions start.\n\nDefenders keep the sign-off, the hash, and the settings list in a place the change process controls. When the standard must change, write a new revision and sign it. Do not let a helpful Friday edit become the silent new baseline. Compare the signed list with the image that will actually be copied, because a document that describes encryption while the image leaves disks open is not yet established. Name an owner who can explain why each major setting is there.\n\nStudents call the first mass deployment the moment the baseline is established. Deployment hands out a standard that should already exist. Students also call a monthly drift report this step. The report is maintenance after copies are in the field. The separating fact is timing. If the approved build is measured and signed before copies go out, as WIN11-CLINIC-2026.03 was on BASE-EST-1, you are looking at establishing the baseline.\n",
        'r0': 'Establishing a baseline writes and signs the approved build before copies go out. WIN11-CLINIC-2026.03 was measured and signed on BASE-EST-1, with the image hash stored, before any employee laptop was issued. The build removes ordinary local admin rights, enables disk encryption, and limits installed software to the clinical list. A benchmark PDF can suggest settings and is not the signed baseline. Deploying the image is the next act. Drift repair is a later act. A one-off click path is a second, unsigned build. Change the baseline only by a new signed revision. Evidence is the signed build and its hash, dated before the first copy left the lab.\n',
        'r1': 'Write the approved setup before you copy it. Build it once from a known-good machine. Measure it and have an owner sign. Do this before the first person receives a copy. Handing copies out is a later step. Pulling drifted machines back is a later step too. A sample checklist can inform the setup. The signed build is the one you will copy. Store the measurement so you can recognize that build. Do not let each technician invent a private setup. The sign-off is the moment this step is real.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a clinic kitchen that writes one recipe card, tastes that batch, and has the owner initial the card before anyone cooks the other fifty pans. The initialed card is the standard. Filling fifty pans is what happens next, and it is not the writing. Tasting a pan a month later and correcting the salt is a third chore. A cookbook on a public shelf can suggest ideas and is not the card the owner signed. In the laptop story, the signed image is that initialed card. The hash stored with the sign-off is the note that lets you recognize the card again, and a private click-path lunch is a pan that never used it.\n',
        'r4': 'Open BASE-EST-1 and record image WIN11-CLINIC-2026.03, the signer, and the hash. Confirm no employee laptop was issued before that sign-off. List the major settings the sign-off covers, including disk encryption and local admin. Do not treat a later mass rollout as this step. Do not treat a drift report as this step. Require a new signed revision before the build quietly changes. Stop when the ticket shows a measured build that existed before copies went out.\n',
        'exam': 'Writing and signing the build before copies go out is this step. Handing the image to new machines is deployment. Pulling drifted machines back is maintenance. A benchmark document is not the signed baseline. A one-off click path is an unsigned second build. The hash should match the build that was signed. The stem to recognize is a known-good build approved before anyone receives it.\n',
    },
    'Deploying a baseline': {
        'body': "Deploying a baseline means a new system starts from the approved build instead of from a one-off series of clicks. The standard already exists. This step is how the next server or laptop receives it. A careful hand-build on a Friday feels productive and creates a machine nobody can match later.\n\nThe mechanism is cloning, imaging, or an automated configuration run that applies the signed build and records the build identity on the new host. The operator should not re-decide firewall rules, local accounts, or installed software during that run. If a setting must differ, the difference is a named exception with an owner, not a memory. Deployment fails when the image used on Thursday is not the image that was signed, even if the project name sounds the same. It also fails when a new hire builds a server from the installer because the imaging server was inconvenient. Establishing the baseline was the sign-off. Hunting drift next month is maintenance. Deployment is the birth of the new system.\n\nServer SRV-441 was cloned from image GOLD-SRV-04 on ticket BASE-DEP-9. The build identifier on the host matches the signed image, including the host firewall and the removal of unused remote services. A second server requested the same week was almost built by hand because the imaging service looked slow. The change stopped that path. The hand-built machine would have been a new snowflake, not a deployment of the baseline. The ticket records the image name, the host name, and the time the clone finished. That trio is the evidence. A verbal claim that the technician followed the checklist is not.\n\nDefenders make the imaging path the easiest path, so the Friday shortcut is more work than doing it right. Check the build identifier after every deployment, before the server is handed to an application team. Alert when a new host appears without that identifier. Keep exceptions listed and expiring. When the signed image is revised, deploy the new revision forward and do not keep silently cloning last year's image because the pipeline still points at it. The pipeline pointer is part of deployment.\n\nStudents describe the writing of the standard and call it deployment. Writing and signing happen before this step. Students also describe a monthly cleanup of drifted servers and call that deployment. Cleanup is maintenance of machines that already exist. The separating fact is the new system's origin. If SRV-441 began as a clone of GOLD-SRV-04 rather than as a click path, the baseline was deployed.\n",
        'r0': "Deploying a baseline starts a new system from the approved build. SRV-441 was cloned from GOLD-SRV-04 on BASE-DEP-9, and the host's build identifier matches that signed image. A Friday hand-build is the failure mode, even when the technician means well. Establishing the baseline was the earlier sign-off. Drift repair is a later check on machines already in service. Exceptions need an owner and an end, not a private click. Point the pipeline at the current signed image after each revision. Check that identifier before an application team receives the host. Evidence is the new host name, the image name, and a matching build identifier.\n",
        'r1': 'New machines should start from the approved build. Cloning an image is the usual path. A Friday spent clicking by hand is the failure. This step uses a setup that was already signed. Inventing the setup is an earlier step. Hunting for drift later is a different step. Check the build name on the new machine. A one-off server will not match the others. Do not treat a wish list as the act of copying. Stop when the new system shows the approved build.\n',
        'r2': "Picture every new exam room stocked from one signed supply card rather than from a nurse's memory on a busy Friday. The card already exists. Stocking the new room is the act of following it. Writing the card last month was a different chore. Walking old rooms next month to fix missing items is a different chore too. A room stocked from memory will not match the card even if the nurse is talented. In the server story, the clone of the signed image is the room stocked from the card. The host that was almost clicked together by hand is the Friday room that would have drifted before it ever opened.\n",
        'r4': 'On BASE-DEP-9, record host SRV-441 and image GOLD-SRV-04. Confirm the build identifier on the host matches the signed image. Do not accept a hand-built server as this deployment. Keep the original sign-off of the image in a separate note. Keep later drift checks in a separate note. Verify the pipeline points at the current image. Stop when the ticket shows a new system that started from the approved build.\n',
        'exam': 'A new system started from the approved build is deployment. Writing and signing that build is the earlier step. Fixing drift on machines already in the field is maintenance. A hand-built Friday server is not deployment of the baseline. The build identifier has to match. An expired image in the pipeline deploys the wrong standard. The stem to recognize is a clone or automated build, not a one-off click path.\n',
    },
    'Maintaining a baseline': {
        'body': 'Maintaining a baseline finds drift and pulls machines back, while the standard itself changes only through the change process. Maintaining a baseline compares live settings with the signed build on a schedule, not only after an incident. Maintaining a baseline treats a host firewall that was turned off as drift to reverse, not as a local improvement. Maintaining a baseline treats an extra admin account the same way, unless an approved exception names it. Maintaining a baseline opens a change when the standard should move, and refuses a quiet edit on one server. Maintaining a baseline records who drifted, what differed, and whether the pull-back succeeded. Maintaining a baseline separates harmless local data from security settings that must match. Maintaining a baseline alerts when a machine misses check-in, because an invisible host is not a compliant host. Maintaining a baseline covers servers, laptops, and the templates that keep spawning new guests. Maintaining a baseline updates the image when the standard changes so tomorrow\'s deploys do not reintroduce the old weakness. Maintaining a baseline does not confuse a failed pull-back with success on a dashboard. Maintaining a baseline gives the owner a ticket when an exception expires and the setting is still loose. Maintaining a baseline scans for new listening services that the build removed. Maintaining a baseline scans for missing disk protection and missing logging. Maintaining a baseline keeps the comparison tool\'s own access narrow and logged. Maintaining a baseline avoids a scan that knocks over a fragile plant system; that estate needs a different maintenance deal. Maintaining a baseline uses maintenance windows where a pull-back would interrupt a person. Maintaining a baseline documents a break-glass path that is still visible after the emergency. Maintaining a baseline removes the break-glass change when the emergency ends, or it becomes the new drift. Maintaining a baseline reports conformance as a percentage only beside the list of actual gaps. Maintaining a baseline feeds repeated gaps back to the people who establish the standard, because the document may be wrong. Maintaining a baseline feeds deploy failures back when new machines arrive already drifted. Maintaining a baseline rechecks contractors\' machines on the same rules if they hold live data. Maintaining a baseline does not accept "we will fix it next quarter" as a closed gap for an exposed admin service. Maintaining a baseline pairs with vulnerability work by turning a known-weak setting back to the agreed value. Maintaining a baseline is not a one-time imaging event. Maintaining a baseline is not a rewrite of the standard done by whoever is on the keyboard tonight. Maintaining a baseline keeps history so a recurring drift points at a tool or a person, not at bad luck. Maintaining a baseline tests the pull-back on a sample machine before running it everywhere. Maintaining a baseline communicates when a pull-back will change what a user feels, such as a removed browser plug-in. Maintaining a baseline includes configuration of network gear if that gear was part of the standard. Maintaining a baseline includes cloud accounts if the standard named those settings. Maintaining a baseline reviews exceptions monthly so temporary openings do not become furniture. Maintaining a baseline writes the label when the evidence is ongoing comparison and correction. Maintaining a baseline chooses a different label when the evidence is the first writing of the build or the first clone. Maintaining a baseline after a breach in a drifted setting, add that setting to the comparison if it was missing. Maintaining a baseline measure how old the oldest drift is, not only how many machines match today. Maintaining a baseline keep a human owner for the report so the numbers have a person who must act. Maintaining a baseline sample a machine from each role every month so a quiet department cannot drift unseen. Maintaining a baseline open a problem record when the same setting drifts on many hosts, because that is a cause not a list of victims. Maintaining a baseline expire local admin rights that were granted for a single install and never removed. Maintaining a baseline compare cloud templates and gold images in the same cycle as laptops. Maintaining a baseline publish the oldest unresolved drift in days, because a fresh failure hides an old one. Maintaining a baseline verify a pull-back survived reboot, since some agents look fixed until the next start. Maintaining a baseline tie repeated firewall disables to the person or tool that keeps turning them off. Maintaining a baseline schedule exception review on a calendar the owners cannot ignore.',
        'r0': 'Maintaining a baseline compares live hosts to the signed build, reverses unapproved drift, and changes the build only through change control. Maintaining a baseline alerts on missed check-ins, expired exceptions, new listeners, and failed pull-backs. Maintaining a baseline updates the deploy image when the standard moves so new machines do not revive old weaknesses. Maintaining a baseline excludes reckless scans of fragile plant gear and uses a window when a correction would interrupt work. Maintaining a baseline is not the original authoring and is not the first-day clone step. Maintaining a baseline sample every role, track the age of the oldest gap, and confirm a pull-back still holds after reboot. Maintaining a baseline escalate repeated drift to a cause, not only to a count of hosts.',
        'r1': 'Maintaining a baseline compares live machines with the agreed build. Maintaining a baseline pulls unapproved changes back. Maintaining a baseline changes the build only through review. Maintaining a baseline treats a disabled firewall as drift. Maintaining a baseline alerts when a machine stops checking in. Maintaining a baseline updates the image after the build changes. Maintaining a baseline is not the first writing of the build. Maintaining a baseline is not the first clone onto new hardware. Maintaining a baseline needs a person who acts on the gaps. Maintaining a baseline tracks how old the oldest gap is.',
        'r2': 'Maintaining a baseline works like tasting the soup at every service and correcting the salt when it wanders. Maintaining a baseline changes the recipe card only when the owner agrees, not when one cook prefers a private version. Maintaining a baseline notices a pot that never comes to the pass, because an unseen pot cannot be tasted. Maintaining a baseline is not agreeing the recipe for the first time, and they are not filling a new pot from the card. Maintaining a baseline writes down which pot drifted and whether the correction held. Maintaining a baseline proves itself when the next service matches the card, or the gap is still on the list.',
        'r4': 'Compare Maintaining a baseline hosts to the signed build on a schedule. Reverse Maintaining a baseline drift that has no approved exception. Change Maintaining a baseline standards only through the change process. Alert Maintaining a baseline misses when a host stops checking in. Update Maintaining a baseline images after the standard itself changes. Report Maintaining a baseline gaps with an owner, not as a percentage alone.',
        'exam': 'Maintaining a baseline is the label when live systems are compared with the build and unapproved drift is pulled back. Maintaining a baseline is not the label for writing that build the first time, or for cloning it onto a new machine. Maintaining a baseline expects the standard to move only through change control, not through a quiet local edit. Maintaining a baseline treats a disabled host firewall or an extra admin as drift unless an exception names it.',
    },
    'Hardening workstations and servers': {
        'body': 'Hardening a workstation or a server means removing what you do not need, patching what you keep, and turning on the controls that live on that host. The machine should offer a smaller, current, better-watched surface than the default install. A policy document can describe the target. Hardening is the state of the computer itself.\n\nUnused services are doors. A file-transfer service nobody uses, or an old clear-text remote login, should be gone rather than merely unadvertised. Patching closes the holes in the software you actually run, on a channel you chose rather than on a random download. Host controls include a local firewall, disk encryption where the machine can leave the building, and ordinary users who are not local administrators. Logging has to be on or the smaller surface is silent. The same idea applies to laptops and to servers, and the details differ: a laptop worries about loss and local admin, while a server worries about listening ports and who can reach them. Hardening a switch, a cloud bucket, or a plant controller is a neighboring topic about a different kind of device.\n\nTicket HARD-16 covers clinic server MED-APP-2 and a nursing laptop. The server no longer listens on port 21, the old file-transfer service is removed, and telnet is gone. The host firewall allows 443 to the application and allows secure shell only from the admin jump host. Automatic updates stay on the approved channel. The laptop has disk encryption on and no everyday local administrator. Both results are recorded as settings you can recheck, not as a slide that says hardened. A request to turn the host firewall off for a vendor demo was filed as a dated exception rather than as a quiet disable.\n\nDefenders recheck listening ports and local admin membership after changes, because hardening rots when a project turns a service back on. Patch compliance belongs in the same review as the removed services. Do not call a machine hardened because it joined the domain. Domain membership is not a host firewall and not a removed service. Separate this work from writing the baseline and from cloning the image. Those steps matter. This label is the reduced, patched, controlled host.\n\nStudents apply this name to a router with a new management password. That work may be necessary and it is hardening of a network device, not of a workstation or a server. Students also stop at a written standard and never look at the listening ports. The separating fact is the kind of machine and the state it is in. If a server or a workstation has lost what it does not need, stays patched, and enforces host controls, HARD-16 is this topic.\n',
        'r0': 'Hardening workstations and servers removes unused functions, patches what remains, and enables host controls. On HARD-16, MED-APP-2 no longer listens on port 21, telnet is removed, and the host firewall allows 443 plus secure shell only from the jump host. Updates come from the approved channel. The nursing laptop has disk encryption and no everyday local administrator. A switch, a cloud bucket, and a plant controller are different device classes. Domain join alone is not a hardened host. Recheck listening ports after later projects. A dated exception is the only careful way to reopen a closed port. Evidence is the removed service, the patch channel, and the host control you can show.\n',
        'r1': 'Remove software and services you do not need. Patch what you decide to keep. Turn on the controls that live on the computer. A server should not keep old remote tools. A workstation user should not be a full administrator. Lock the disk on machines that leave the building. This work is about those computers, not about routers. A cloud bucket is a different job. A plant controller is a different job. Joining a directory does not finish the work. Write down what you removed and what you turned on.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a clinic workbench. You clear the tools nobody uses, you oil and tighten the ones you keep, and you lock the drawer. A sign that says the bench should be tidy is not the cleared bench. Ordering a second identical bench from a photo is not the same as having cleared this one. The lock on the network closet down the hall is a different room entirely. In the ticket, the server that lost the unused file-transfer tool and kept a host firewall is that cleared bench. The laptop with the disk locked and without everyday admin rights is the drawer that actually shuts.\n',
        'r4': 'On HARD-16, list the services removed from MED-APP-2, including the listener on port 21. Record the host firewall allows and the update channel. Record disk encryption and local admin state on the nursing laptop. Do not mark the host done because it joined the directory. Do not use this label for a switch or a plant controller. Recheck after the next project that might re-enable a service. Stop when the ticket shows a smaller, patched, controlled workstation or server.\n',
        'exam': "Unused services removed, patches current, and host controls on is this work. A router or switch is a different hardening topic. A cloud bucket is a different topic. A plant controller is a different topic. A written standard is not the host's state. Domain membership is not a host firewall. The stem to recognize is a workstation or server with a smaller, current, enforced surface.\n",
    },
    'Hardening network devices': {
        'body': 'Hardening network devices starts with a current firmware image, a changed default secret, and management that is reachable only from the management network. Hardening network devices shuts unused switch interfaces so an open jack in a lobby is not a free path onto the network. Hardening network devices disables old remote protocols that send secrets in the clear, and keeps an encrypted admin path. Hardening network devices replaces a shared device password with named admin accounts that can be revoked. Hardening network devices sends device logs to the central store and timestamps them. Hardening network devices uses 802.1X where a port or a wireless attachment must authenticate the user or the device before it passes traffic. Hardening network devices names the three 802.1X roles: the supplicant on the endpoint, the authenticator on the switch or radio, and the authentication server. Hardening network devices keeps that authentication server able to say no, or 802.1X becomes a formality. Hardening network devices prefers certificate-based methods inside 802.1X when devices can hold a certificate. Hardening network devices does not confuse 802.1X with a pre-shared wifi passphrase, which is a different and weaker door for a shared secret. Hardening network devices can still use interface stickiness or a limit on learned addresses as a smaller control where 802.1X is not deployed. Hardening network devices documents which interfaces are intentionally open, such as a printer that cannot speak 802.1X, and isolates them. Hardening network devices turns off unused routing features and unused management services. Hardening network devices protects the console and the out-of-band path with the same seriousness as the remote path. Hardening network devices backs up the device configuration to a protected store after each approved change. Hardening network devices compares the running configuration with the approved one during maintenance. Hardening network devices separates this work from host firewalls on servers, which do not configure the switch. Hardening network devices separates this work from cloud security groups, which are a provider construct. Hardening network devices banners the device with the notice the organization requires. Hardening network devices restricts who can change spanning-tree and routing so a desk jack cannot become a new gateway. Hardening network devices monitors for a new device claiming to be a gateway or a dynamic address server. Hardening network devices sets a login banner and an idle timeout on admin sessions. Hardening network devices removes the vendor\'s published secret from every existing box, not only from the standard. Hardening network devices tests that a shut interface stays shut after a reload. Hardening network devices tests that management is unreachable from a user subnet. Hardening network devices matches a ticket whose facts show a switch, router, or similar box being tightened. Hardening network devices is the wrong label for a laptop image or a storage-bucket policy. Hardening network devices includes wireless controllers when they are the management brain for the radios. Hardening network devices keeps the control-plane protected from user traffic floods where the platform allows it. Hardening network devices records the firmware version the standard requires and alerts when a device falls behind. Hardening network devices does not run a reckless scan against a device that sits in a safety network; coordinate that test. Hardening network devices pairs with the baseline so a replaced switch is configured from the standard, not from memory. Hardening network devices set the clock from an internal time source so device logs line up with server logs. Hardening network devices limit which addresses may start a management session, and log the denied attempts. Hardening network devices replace community strings that were shipped with the product and stop using clear-text polling. Hardening network devices document a printer or badge reader that cannot authenticate, and park it on an isolated segment. Hardening network devices test that a user laptop in a conference room cannot open the device\'s admin page. Hardening network devices keep a spare configured device for the core, so a failure is a swap rather than a midnight rebuild from memory. Hardening network devices disable unused dynamic routing on access switches that should only forward inside one subnet. Hardening network devices record the last successful login and alarm on a login from outside the management range. Hardening network devices verify neighbor discovery cannot be used from a desk jack to impersonate a gateway. Hardening network devices export the configuration after the change window and diff it against the ticket. Hardening network devices teach the desk not to "temporarily" enable an old protocol for a vendor demo. Hardening network devices review interface descriptions so an unlabeled jack is treated as unused and shut.',
        'r0': 'Hardening network devices demand current firmware, named admin accounts, management only from the management network, and shut unused interfaces. Hardening network devices deploy 802.1X with a supplicant, an authenticator, and an authentication server that can refuse. Hardening network devices treat certificate methods inside 802.1X as stronger than a shared wifi passphrase taped to a wall. Hardening network devices back up configs, compare them to the standard, and keep old clear-text admin protocols disabled. Hardening network devices are not host firewalls and are not cloud security groups. Hardening network devices sync device clocks, restrict management sources, and diff the saved configuration against the ticket. Hardening network devices isolate gear that cannot authenticate and alarm on admin logins from outside the management range.',
        'r1': 'Hardening network devices update firmware and change default secrets. Hardening network devices shut unused interfaces. Hardening network devices manage devices only from the management network. Hardening network devices disable clear-text admin protocols. Hardening network devices use 802.1X before traffic is allowed. Hardening network devices name supplicant, authenticator, and server. Hardening network devices prefer certificates over a shared passphrase. Hardening network devices back up the configuration after changes. Hardening network devices are not a laptop hardening job. Hardening network devices are not a cloud bucket policy.',
        'r2': "Hardening network devices works like rekeying a building's service doors and barring the doors nobody uses. Hardening network devices lets a badge decide who may pass a door, which is the idea behind authenticated attachment. Hardening network devices does not leave the master key on the vendor's packing slip. Hardening network devices keeps the office that programs the doors off the public hallway. Hardening network devices is not tidying a person's workbench, and they are not locking a rented cloud locker. Hardening network devices proves itself when an unknown visitor cannot plug in and walk into the office network. Hardening network devices checks the barred door again after a storm, because a latch can look closed and still be loose.",
        'r4': 'Update Hardening network devices firmware and remove vendor default secrets. Shut Hardening network devices interfaces that have no documented need. Limit Hardening network devices management to the management network. Enable Hardening network devices 802.1X with a server that can refuse. Backup Hardening network devices configurations after every approved change. Separate Hardening network devices tasks from host firewalls and cloud policies.',
        'exam': 'Hardening network devices is the label when a switch or router loses default secrets, old admin protocols, and unused interfaces. Hardening network devices expects 802.1X when the stem describes a port that stays closed until a user or device is authenticated. Hardening network devices names supplicant, authenticator, and authentication server as the three roles. Hardening network devices is not the label for a server image or a cloud storage policy.',
    },
    'Hardening cloud infrastructure': {
        'body': "Hardening cloud infrastructure tightens roles so a person or a service can do one job, not every job in the account. Hardening cloud infrastructure keeps storage private unless a named business need publishes a specific object. Hardening cloud infrastructure turns on the audit log for admin actions and protects that log from the admins it watches. Hardening cloud infrastructure removes open management paths from the internet, including shells and dashboards that do not need to be public. Hardening cloud infrastructure requires a second factor on the root or owner account and keeps that account unused for daily work. Hardening cloud infrastructure replaces long-lived keys with short-lived credentials where the platform allows it. Hardening cloud infrastructure separates production, test, and logging into boundaries that a single stolen key cannot cross. Hardening cloud infrastructure checks security groups and firewall constructs for a wide-open inbound rule and closes it. Hardening cloud infrastructure encrypts volumes and object stores with keys the organization can control or rotate. Hardening cloud infrastructure blocks public snapshot sharing and public image sharing by default. Hardening cloud infrastructure enables detection for a bucket or container that becomes world-readable. Hardening cloud infrastructure writes infrastructure as code so a hardened account can be rebuilt and reviewed. Hardening cloud infrastructure reviews that code in the same change process as a firewall rule. Hardening cloud infrastructure separates this work from a laptop baseline and from a switch firmware upgrade. Hardening cloud infrastructure does not assume the provider's default project is private. Hardening cloud infrastructure names an owner for each account so a departed employee does not remain the only admin. Hardening cloud infrastructure removes unused credentials and unused regions that nobody monitors. Hardening cloud infrastructure logs data-access events for the stores that hold sensitive records, not only login events. Hardening cloud infrastructure sets a budget alarm so a surprise crypto-mining guest is visible as spend. Hardening cloud infrastructure applies the same standard to serverless functions and to managed databases, not only to virtual machines. Hardening cloud infrastructure restricts which internet addresses may reach an admin plane if it must be reachable at all. Hardening cloud infrastructure prefers a private network path for administration. Hardening cloud infrastructure tests the standard with a tool that flags a public bucket, an open group, and a missing log. Hardening cloud infrastructure treats a finding as drift to reverse, in the same spirit as a host that left the baseline. Hardening cloud infrastructure is the label when the evidence is cloud roles, storage exposure, and admin logging. Hardening cloud infrastructure is the wrong label for a switch interface or a workstation image. Hardening cloud infrastructure documents the shared-responsibility line so the team hardens what the provider does not operate for them. Hardening cloud infrastructure rotates keys that were pasted into a ticket or a chat. Hardening cloud infrastructure prevents a deployment pipeline from holding a permanent all-powerful key. Hardening cloud infrastructure reviews third-party connections into the account and removes the ones a project abandoned. Hardening cloud infrastructure turn on drift detection for accounts that were built by hand before the templates existed. Hardening cloud infrastructure block users from creating their own keys that never expire and never rotate. Hardening cloud infrastructure require approval before a security group allows the world to reach an admin port number the platform exposes. Hardening cloud infrastructure separate the pipeline identity from human identities so a build cannot browse mail. Hardening cloud infrastructure log object deletes on the sensitive stores and alert when a mass delete starts. Hardening cloud infrastructure deny regions the organization does not use so a surprise workload cannot hide there. Hardening cloud infrastructure review shared images and revoke public launch permissions left from a tutorial. Hardening cloud infrastructure set a maximum session length for the web console. Hardening cloud infrastructure inventory third-party marketplace subscriptions and remove the ones with standing access. Hardening cloud infrastructure apply the same private-storage rule to backups the platform stores in another account. Hardening cloud infrastructure test a restore of the audit log into a restricted account so the log is not only written but readable later. Hardening cloud infrastructure name a break-glass account, lock it, alarm on its use, and rotate it after every use. Hardening cloud infrastructure prohibit access keys in source code by scanning the repository on every change. Hardening cloud infrastructure document which data may sit in which region before a project picks a location for convenience. Hardening cloud infrastructure require encryption in transit for the load balancer or the object endpoint that clients use. Hardening cloud infrastructure revisit service accounts quarterly and delete the ones whose workload is gone.",
        'r0': 'Hardening cloud infrastructure shrink roles, keep storage private, log admin and data access, and close management paths from the internet. Hardening cloud infrastructure protect the owner account with a second factor, prefer short-lived credentials, and alarm on spend. Hardening cloud infrastructure encode the account in reviewable templates and scan for public buckets and wide-open inbound rules. Hardening cloud infrastructure are not switch hardening and are not a workstation image. Hardening cloud infrastructure include functions and managed databases, not only virtual machines. Hardening cloud infrastructure alarm on break-glass use, block non-expiring personal keys, and scan repositories for pasted secrets. Hardening cloud infrastructure deny unused regions and review marketplace subscriptions that still hold standing access.',
        'r1': 'Hardening cloud infrastructure shrink roles to one job. Hardening cloud infrastructure keep storage private by default. Hardening cloud infrastructure log admin actions. Hardening cloud infrastructure close open management from the internet. Hardening cloud infrastructure protect the owner account with a second factor. Hardening cloud infrastructure prefer short-lived credentials. Hardening cloud infrastructure alarm on surprise spend. Hardening cloud infrastructure scan for public buckets. Hardening cloud infrastructure are not switch firmware work. Hardening cloud infrastructure are not a laptop image. Hardening cloud infrastructure alarm when the break-glass account is used. Hardening cloud infrastructure scan code for pasted secrets.',
        'r2': 'Hardening cloud infrastructure works like a rented cage in a side hall, door latched, with the cash-box key on a very short loop. Hardening cloud infrastructure gives each helper a key that opens one cabinet, not the whole building. Hardening cloud infrastructure writes down who opened a cabinet, and keep that book where the helper cannot edit it. Hardening cloud infrastructure is not rekeying the office switch closet, and they are not clearing a personal workbench. Hardening cloud infrastructure notices if that door was left open, and close it the same day. Hardening cloud infrastructure proves itself when a stranger on the street cannot browse the shelves or the account books.',
        'r4': 'Shrink Hardening cloud infrastructure roles to the single job each identity needs. Block Hardening cloud infrastructure public storage unless a named need says otherwise. Enable Hardening cloud infrastructure audit logs and protect them from the admins they watch. Close Hardening cloud infrastructure management paths that face the open internet. Scan Hardening cloud infrastructure accounts for wide-open inbound rules and public snapshots. Separate Hardening cloud infrastructure work from switch firmware and laptop images.',
        'exam': 'Hardening cloud infrastructure fits a question centered on cloud roles, private storage, and logged admin actions. Hardening cloud infrastructure is a poor fit for a laptop build, a switch firmware task, or a radio coverage walk. Hardening cloud infrastructure expects a locked-down owner account, short-lived credentials, and no accidental public bucket. Hardening cloud infrastructure treats a wide-open management path from the internet as a finding to close.',
    },
    'Hardening operational technology': {
        'body': 'Hardening operational technology protects plant and building-control gear without knocking it over, because a careless test can stop a real process. Hardening operational technology segments that gear away from ordinary office networks and away from guest wireless. Hardening operational technology gives change rights only to the people who run the process, through the plant procedure. Hardening operational technology refuses a reckless vulnerability scan that the vendor and the safety owner have not approved. Hardening operational technology prefers passive watching when an active probe could reset a controller. Hardening operational technology documents which protocols the process actually needs and blocks the rest at the boundary. Hardening operational technology treats a remote vendor connection as a temporary, logged path, not as an always-on tunnel. Hardening operational technology keeps engineering workstations cleaner than general laptops, because those stations program the controllers. Hardening operational technology patches only in the maintenance window the plant allows, and with a tested backout. Hardening operational technology does not assume a server baseline can be copied onto a controller that was never designed for it. Hardening operational technology separates this work from a wireless survey, which measures signal, and from a heat map, which draws it. Hardening operational technology names safety as a constraint beside confidentiality: availability of the process can outrank a hurried patch. Hardening operational technology records the firmware the vendor supports, because an unsupported jump can brick a line. Hardening operational technology inventories the controllers, the historians, and the human-machine screens so nothing is invisible. Hardening operational technology puts a monitored boundary between the plant and the enterprise, and watches that boundary. Hardening operational technology disallows direct reachability from a guest network to a pump, a badge controller, or a boiler. Hardening operational technology uses unique credentials per vendor, revoked when the contract ends. Hardening operational technology stores backups of controller logic where a failure can be restored by someone who has practiced it. Hardening operational technology tests restores in a lab or a scheduled outage, not by experimenting on a live line. Hardening operational technology limits USB and laptop attachments on the plant floor. Hardening operational technology explains to responders that unplugging a controller is not the same as isolating a mail server. Hardening operational technology is the label when the evidence is safety-sensitive equipment and a careful, segmented change. Hardening operational technology is the wrong label for an office laptop standard or a cloud bucket. Hardening operational technology includes building management systems when they control physical plant, not only factory lines. Hardening operational technology reviews firewall rules toward the plant after any project that "just needed a temporary hole". Hardening operational technology removes that hole when the project ends. Hardening operational technology trains the on-call technology staff not to run office incident steps blindly against the plant. Hardening operational technology coordinates detection content with someone who knows which alerts would be normal process noise. Hardening operational technology writes the allowed-change calendar where both operations and technology can read it. Hardening operational technology keeps a paper or out-of-band way to reach the plant operator if the enterprise network is the thing that failed. Hardening operational technology map every conduit between the plant and the enterprise and label which one is supposed to exist. Hardening operational technology put a jump host in that conduit so administrators do not connect straight from a mail-reading laptop. Hardening operational technology record process-safe maintenance windows on the same calendar as production change windows. Hardening operational technology keep spare controller media offline so a network worm cannot encrypt the only logic backup. Hardening operational technology identify which alerts would page a technician versus which would page an operator. Hardening operational technology write a joint procedure for a suspected compromise that does not tell technology staff to power off a live line. Hardening operational technology review wireless sensors separately from the office wireless survey, because a sensor network is still plant gear. Hardening operational technology prohibit dual-homed engineering laptops that sit on the plant network and the internet at the same time. Hardening operational technology test an account revocation for a contractor and confirm the remote path actually closes. Hardening operational technology store the vendor support agreement next to the firmware standard so an emergency call has a number. Hardening operational technology photograph cabinet layouts so a replacement module goes back in the same place. Hardening operational technology include building-badge controllers and HVAC panels if they can unlock doors or stop cooling. Hardening operational technology run a table conversation with operators before any technical drill that might touch the line.',
        'r0': 'Hardening operational technology segment plant and building controls from office and guest networks, and change them only through the plant procedure. Hardening operational technology ban unapproved active scans, prefer passive observation, and patch only inside a tested maintenance window. Hardening operational technology treat vendor remote access as temporary and logged, with unique credentials that end with the contract. Hardening operational technology are not a wireless coverage survey and are not an office workstation image. Hardening operational technology put process safety ahead of a hurried patch, and practice logic restores off the live line. Hardening operational technology force administration through a jump host and keep logic backups offline from the enterprise network. Hardening operational technology ban dual-homed engineering laptops and confirm contractor paths actually close when accounts end.',
        'r1': 'Hardening operational technology segment plant gear from office networks. Hardening operational technology block guest wireless from pumps and boilers. Hardening operational technology change devices only through the plant procedure. Hardening operational technology skip reckless scans. Hardening operational technology patch only in an approved window. Hardening operational technology log vendor remote access and then remove it. Hardening operational technology back up controller logic and practice a restore. Hardening operational technology are not a signal survey. Hardening operational technology are not a laptop image. Hardening operational technology put safety ahead of a rushed patch.',
        'r2': "Hardening operational technology works like locking a greenhouse control shed without kicking the irrigation valves to see if they rattle. Hardening operational technology keeps the shed off the public path, and give the key only to the grower who knows the crop. Hardening operational technology refuses a stranger's test that would flood the beds just to prove the lock. Hardening operational technology is not walking the dining room to see where the light is bright. Hardening operational technology is not drawing a picture of that brightness. Hardening operational technology proves itself when a guest cannot reach the valves, and the crop still gets water.",
        'r4': "Segment Hardening operational technology networks away from guest wireless and ordinary office nets. Approve Hardening operational technology scans with the safety owner before they run. Patch Hardening operational technology gear only inside the plant's maintenance window. Expire Hardening operational technology vendor tunnels when the work ends. Practice Hardening operational technology restores of controller logic off the live line. Separate Hardening operational technology tasks from radio surveys and heat maps.",
        'exam': 'Hardening operational technology fits a question centered on plant or building-control gear, segmentation, and careful change. Hardening operational technology is not the label for a wireless survey, a heat map, or a normal office image. Hardening operational technology expects no reckless scan, a maintenance window, and no standing vendor tunnel. Hardening operational technology puts process safety beside the technical control, because a careless test can stop the line.',
    },
    'Wireless site surveys': {
        'body': "A wireless site survey measures real signal, interference, and coverage in the building before you decide where radios belong. It replaces a guess from the hallway. The product is evidence: where the signal is strong, where it dies, and what else is shouting on the same channels.\n\nYou walk the rooms with a measuring tool, or you associate and move data while you walk, and you record the results against a floor plan. A passive walk listens to what is already in the air. An active walk joins the network and shows whether a chart can actually load. A predictive drawing from a floor plan, with no walk, is a model. It can start a conversation and it can miss a lead-lined room. A heat map is the picture of the measurements, not the survey itself. Placement is the later decision about where to bolt a radio. Survey and placement support each other, and they are not the same sentence. If you have only a hunch, you have neither.\n\nThe clinic walk for ticket WIFI-SUR-4 found radiology at about -82 dBm with a signal-to-noise ratio too low for reliable chart use. A glance from the hallway had called that corridor fine. The lead lining, not the hallway, was the fact. The survey also marked two access points on the same channel at the east nurses' station, which explained retries the desk had blamed on the application. Those two findings came from measurement. No radio was moved during the walk. The move belongs to a later change once the picture is trusted.\n\nDefenders survey before buying a guess's worth of hardware, and they survey again after walls or lead shielding change. Save the measurements with the floor plan so the next person does not restart from folklore. Include the rooms where people actually work, not only the corridors that are easy to walk. Separate the heat map from the decision to mount a radio. When a vendor offers a desktop prediction as if it were a completed survey, ask whether anyone measured radiology. If the answer is no, the dead zone is still a rumor you have not tested.\n\nStudents answer with access-point placement when the stem only shows a measurement of a dead zone. The measurement is the survey. Students call a colorful prediction a survey when nobody walked. The separating fact is the measurement in the building. WIFI-SUR-4 is a survey because radiology was measured, the dead zone was unexpected, and the bolts had not moved yet.\n",
        'r0': 'A site survey measures signal, interference, and coverage in the building before placement. WIFI-SUR-4 recorded radiology near -82 dBm with a signal-to-noise ratio too low for chart use, a dead zone a hallway guess missed. An active survey associates and moves data. A passive survey listens. A predictive floor-plan drawing is not a completed walk. A heat map pictures the results and is not the walk itself. Placement is the later bolt decision. Remeasure after shielding or walls change. Save the floor plan with those readings so the next walk does not restart from folklore. Evidence is the unexpected weak spot, with numbers, captured before radios are moved.\n',
        'r1': 'Measure the building before you hang the radios. Walk the rooms and record the real signal. A hallway guess can miss a dead zone. Interference belongs in the same walk. A drawing from a floor plan is only a model. A colored map is the picture of the measurements. Where you bolt the radio comes later. Walk the rooms where people actually work. Write down the weak spot you did not expect. Do not buy hardware from a hunch. Measure again after the walls change.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a nurse walking the ward with a light meter before anyone hangs lamps. The meter shows a dim procedure room that looked fine from the corridor, because the door and the lining were doing something the corridor could not reveal. A sketch drawn from the blueprints, with no walk, is a guess on paper. The colored sketch you make after the walk is a picture of the readings, not the walk itself. Deciding which hook gets a lamp comes after. In the wireless work, the radiology measurement is that meter reading. The dead zone nobody expected is the dim room, and the radios stay unmoved until the reading is trusted.\n',
        'r4': "Open WIFI-SUR-4 and record the radiology signal reading and the weak signal-to-noise note. Note that the hallway guess missed it. Save the same-channel overlap at the east nurses' station as interference, not as a bolt decision. Do not call a floor-plan prediction a finished survey. Do not call a heat map the walk. Do not move radios in this ticket. Stop when the ticket shows measurements taken in the rooms before placement.\n",
        'exam': 'Measurement in the building is the survey. A hallway guess is not. A predictive drawing without a walk is not a finished survey. A heat map is the picture of results, not the survey itself. Placement is where the radio goes after you know. The stem to recognize is an unexpected dead zone or interference reading captured before the bolts move.\n',
    },
    'Heat maps': {
        'body': 'Heat maps draw measured signal as color or contour so strong, weak, and overlapping areas are visible at a glance. Heat maps show coverage holes that a spreadsheet of numbers hides. Heat maps show two radios colliding on one channel along the same corridor, which a walk without a picture may understate. Heat maps are a view of survey data, not a substitute for the walk that collected it. Heat maps must say which band, which floor, and which date the colors represent. Heat maps mislead when the scale is stretched so a mediocre signal looks bright. Heat maps mislead when the map is a vendor prediction that nobody checked with a client. Heat maps should overlay the floor plan people recognize, including rooms that were locked during the walk. Heat maps should mark predicted versus measured so a designer cannot confuse the two. Heat maps help place a second radio only after the overlap is understood, because overlap can be harmful or useful. Heat maps help explain a complaint: a user in a red zone has evidence, not a vibe. Heat maps do not, by themselves, choose encryption: a bright blob can still be an old handshake. Heat maps call out a bright staff area that is still not WPA3, because brightness is not a modern lock. Heat maps do not replace 802.1X, because a well-colored room can still share one passphrase. Heat maps differ from the survey, which is the measurement activity, and from placement, which is where hardware goes. Heat maps differ from plant segmentation, which is not a picture of wireless brightness. Heat maps include the legend so a reader knows what color means in the unit you measured. Heat maps include client type if the map was built from a laptop that hears better than the handhelds. Heat maps get updated when walls move, or the picture becomes a historical poster. Heat maps are the label when the evidence is a picture of strong and weak signal and of overlap. Heat maps are the wrong label for the walking measurement itself or for the mounting screws. Heat maps export in a form the next designer can open, not as a single slide nobody can interrogate. Heat maps compare before and after a change so the project can show the hole closed. Heat maps note external spill: a bright parking area may be coverage you did not intend to offer. Heat maps keep the map with the survey date so an argument next year has a source. Heat maps use the same color scale on every floor so a reader does not think one wing is healthier only because the legend changed. Heat maps pin the map to the survey file that produced it, including the tool version. Heat maps draw both bands when clients use both, or label clearly that one band is missing. Heat maps mark the location of each radio as a point so overlap can be traced to hardware, not only to color. Heat maps show a second view for the handheld devices if they hear less than the survey laptop. Heat maps call out a corridor that looks fine at standing height and fails at a cart or a bed. Heat maps include an outdoor view when spill is the complaint, with the building edge drawn. Heat maps avoid smoothing that paints over a real hole between two sample points. Heat maps store the map where facilities and technology can both open it during a complaint. Heat maps annotate construction zones so a pale area is not mistaken for a radio failure. Heat maps compare a map after power was lowered with the map from before, using the same scale. Heat maps highlight same-channel overlap separately from raw brightness so the two problems are not one blob. Heat maps note where roaming should work between floors and where the picture shows a cliff. Heat maps keep a small inset of the legend in the image itself, not only in a slide note that gets stripped. Heat maps reject a map whose date is older than the last renovation. Heat maps use the picture in the design review, not as a poster that nobody is allowed to question. Heat maps record the name of the person who signs that the picture matches the walk. Heat maps export the underlying points if the tool allows, so a later engineer is not stuck with a flat image. Heat maps tie each pale zone to a decision: add a radio, move a radio, or accept the hole.',
        'r0': 'Heat maps render survey measurements as a scaled picture of strength, holes, and co-channel overlap, with band and date. Heat maps separate prediction from measurement, and keep a legend a stranger can read. Heat maps do not treat a bright color as WPA3, and do not treat color as a substitute for 802.1X. Heat maps are not the walk that collected the numbers and are not the physical mounting choice. Heat maps show unintended spill, such as a bright area outside the building, as a design fact. Heat maps keep one color scale, both bands or a clear omission, and radio points that explain the overlap. Heat maps export underlying points when the tool allows, and retire pictures older than the last renovation.',
        'r1': 'Heat maps picture where signal is strong or weak. Heat maps show holes and same-channel overlap. Heat maps need a legend, a band, and a date. Heat maps must not pretend a prediction was measured. Heat maps do not make a bright spot into WPA3. Heat maps do not replace per-person sign-in. Heat maps are not the walk itself. Heat maps are not the mounting decision. Heat maps should show spill outside the building. Heat maps need a refresh when walls move.',
        'r2': "Heat maps work like a rainfall painting of the garden, deep color where water landed and pale color where it did not. Heat maps need a key so deep color means a real amount, not an artist's mood. Heat maps show two sprinklers flooding one bed while another bed stays pale. Heat maps do not pretend the painting is a new lock on the shed. Heat maps are not the walk with the moisture meter, and they are not the stake in the soil. Heat maps prove themselves when a gardener can point at a pale bed and a flooded bed without retelling the whole walk.",
        'r4': 'Label Heat maps with band, floor, date, and a legend. Separate Heat maps predictions from measurements. Show Heat maps overlap and holes, not only the bright middle. Mark Heat maps spill beyond the building. Refuse Heat maps brightness as proof of WPA3 or of per-person sign-in. Refresh Heat maps after walls or furniture move. Store Heat maps with the survey file and the tool version that produced them.',
        'exam': 'Heat maps is the label when the stem shows a picture of strong, weak, and overlapping signal. Heat maps is not the label for the walk that measured the building, or for where the radio was screwed in. Heat maps does not prove WPA3 or 802.1X, because color is coverage, not authentication. Heat maps expects a legend, a band, and a date before the picture is trusted.',
    },
    'Access-point placement': {
        'body': "Access-point placement puts the radios where people work, away from metal and electrical noise, and not in a spot that spills into the street more than it must. The survey told you how the building behaves. Placement is the choice of the bolt hole, the antenna aim, and the power that follows from that choice.\n\nMetal, elevators, and lead eat or reflect signal. A radio buried in a metal ceiling plenum serves the plenum better than the nurses. Microwaves and other radios on the same channel add noise, so the spot and the channel plan belong together. Coverage should overlap enough for a cart to roam, without every radio shouting on one channel in one hall. Power that is higher than the room needs will light the parking lot. That spill is both a performance mistake and an exposure, because someone outside can hear a network that was meant for the ward. Directional antennas and lower power are placement tools. They are not a substitute for having measured the room first.\n\nTicket WIFI-PLC-6 moved one clinic radio from inside a metal ceiling to a hallway mount outside radiology. After the move, the parking-lot reading fell from about -48 dBm, which was easily usable, to about -78 dBm, which was much harder to use from a car. Inside the hallway, the previously dead approach to radiology improved enough for chart use. The survey ticket had already recorded the dead zone. This ticket is the relocation. The installer also dropped the transmit power one step so the lot would not climb back to a comfortable reading. Both the mount and the power change are placement.\n\nDefenders check the spot after the install, not only on the work order. Walk the parking lot and the public sidewalk with the same kind of measurement the survey used. Reject mounts chosen because a ceiling tile was easy to open. Keep guest and clinical radios from covering the street just to simplify a drawing. When a remodel adds metal, revisit the bolts. Do not answer a placement problem by only renaming the network. The name does not move the energy.\n\nStudents describe the survey numbers and stop, as if measuring radiology were the same as moving the radio. The numbers justify the move. The move is this topic. Students also place a radio for the installer's convenience and call the spill unavoidable. The separating fact is the physical spot and what that spot radiates. WIFI-PLC-6 is placement because the radio left the metal ceiling, the hallway improved, and the parking lot got quieter.\n",
        'r0': 'Access-point placement chooses the mount, the aim, and the power so people have signal and the street has less. WIFI-PLC-6 moved a radio from a metal ceiling to a hallway mount. Parking-lot signal fell from about -48 dBm to about -78 dBm, and the hallway approach to radiology became usable. Transmit power was lowered one step so the lot would not recover. The earlier survey measured the dead zone and did not move the bolt. Metal, lead, and same-channel neighbors belong in the placement choice. Recheck the sidewalk after install. Evidence is the old spot, the new spot, and the spill reading.\n',
        'r1': 'Put the radio where people work. Keep it away from metal and noisy gear. Do not spill into the street more than you must. A metal ceiling can swallow the signal. A window can feed the parking lot. Measuring the rooms comes first. Choosing the bolt hole is this step. Lower the power if the lot can still join. Check the spot again after the move. An easy ceiling tile is a weak reason. Record the old spot and the new spot.\n',
        'r2': "Picture hanging a lamp over a nurses' station instead of inside a metal supply cabinet, and tilting the shade so it does not glare into the parking lot. The cabinet mount wastes the light on the cabinet. The window glare tells the lot more than the ward needs. Walking the ward with a meter last week told you the procedure room was dim. Hanging the lamp is the later act. In the wireless change, the hallway mount is the lamp over the station. The drop in parking-lot strength is the shade finally pointed inward, and the metal ceiling was the cabinet you stopped using.\n",
        'r4': 'On WIFI-PLC-6, record the old metal-ceiling mount and the new hallway mount. Record the parking-lot readings before and after, about -48 dBm and about -78 dBm. Note the power step-down. Do not file this as the measurement walk. Walk the lot once after the change and attach that reading. Do not accept a mount chosen only because the tile was easy. Stop when the ticket shows where the radio went and how far the signal spills.\n',
        'exam': 'The bolt hole, the aim, and the power are placement. The measurement walk is the survey. A metal ceiling is a poor mount. Parking-lot spill is part of the decision, not a trivia note. A heat map does not move a radio. Renaming the network does not move it either. The stem to recognize is a radio relocated, with the outside signal going down and the work area going up.\n',
    },
    'Mobile device management': {
        'body': "Mobile device management is the control plane for phones and tablets the organization is allowed to manage. From a console you push settings and applications, and you can lock or wipe a device that is lost or leaving. It is the mechanism. Who owns the phone is a separate decision that changes how hard you may push.\n\nEnrollment puts the handset under that console. A configuration profile can require a screen lock, encryption, or a minimum operating-system level before mail is allowed to appear. Applications can be required or forbidden. When a device is reported lost, the console can lock it immediately and, depending on the ownership rule, wipe company data or the whole device. The action is remote. Nobody needs the handset in the room. A directory password policy on a desktop computer does not do this. It never sees the phone. Ownership models decide the wipe's scope. This topic is the console and the commands, whether the phone is personal, company-owned, or chosen from a catalog.\n\nTicket MDM-27 starts at 09:40, when a nurse reported a clinic-managed phone missing from an exam room. At 10:05 the same morning the console showed the lock command accepted. Mail stopped presenting on that handset before the end of the hour. The ticket records the device identifier, the reporter, the lock time, and the console operator. A wipe was held because the ownership rule for that class of phone required a second approval. The hold is still management: the console could do it, and the rule said when. A help-desk note that only reset a desktop password would not have locked the phone.\n\nDefenders enroll before company data arrives, test lock and wipe on a lab handset, and watch for devices that check in and then fall out of compliance. Alert when a required encryption setting is off. Time the path from a loss report to the lock so a same-hour goal is real. Separate the console procedure from the policy that says which ownership model you allow. Do not leave a departed person's phone enrolled and forgotten. The same console that locks a loss should retire a device when the person leaves.\n\nStudents call any phone rule by this name, including a poster that says do not lose the phone. The poster is an instruction. Students also answer with the ownership label when the stem shows a console lock. Ownership explains whose phone it is. The separating fact is the remote control. MDM-27 is this topic because a console locked the handset at 10:05, the same hour it was reported.\n",
        'r0': 'Mobile device management pushes settings, apps, and remote lock or wipe to enrolled phones and tablets. MDM-27 locked a missing handset at 10:05 after a 09:40 report, and mail stopped the same hour. Profiles can require a screen lock, encryption, or a minimum system version before mail appears. A desktop password policy never sees the phone. Ownership decides whether a wipe is partial or whole. Test lock and wipe in a lab. Retire devices when people leave. Time the path from the loss report to the accepted lock so a same-hour goal is real. Evidence is the console command, the device identifier, and the time it was accepted.\n',
        'r1': 'This console pushes settings to phones and tablets. It can push approved apps too. It can lock a lost device from afar. It can erase company data from afar. The phone has to be one you are allowed to manage. Who owns the phone is a separate question. A password rule on a desk computer is not this console. Lock a reported loss the same day. Keep a record of the lock or the erase. Enroll the phone before company data arrives.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a dispatch desk that can lock a clinic vehicle and, if the rule allows, empty its cargo box without holding the keys. The desk is the control. The question of who paid for the vehicle changes how empty you are allowed to make it, and it does not remove the desk. A poster in the garage that says lock your doors is not the desk. Resetting a building alarm code does not lock the vehicle in the lot. In the phone story, the console is that dispatch desk. The lock accepted at mid-morning is the vehicle locked after the loss call, with the cargo decision waiting on the ownership rule.\n',
        'r4': 'On MDM-27, record the 09:40 loss report and the 10:05 lock. Save the device identifier and the console operator. Note why a wipe was held for a second approval. Do not substitute a desktop password reset for the phone lock. Do not answer with only the ownership label. Confirm the handset was enrolled before mail was allowed. Stop when the ticket shows a remote command that the console actually sent.\n',
        'exam': 'The console that pushes settings and can lock or wipe is this control. A desktop password policy is not. A poster about lost phones is not. Who owns the device is a separate label. Partial wipe versus full wipe follows that ownership rule. Enrollment comes before company data. The stem to recognize is a remote lock or wipe from the management console, timed against the loss report.\n',
    },
    'Bring-your-own-device': {
        'body': "Bring-your-own-device means the person owns the phone. The organization still needs a rule for company data that lands on it. Ownership does not become corporate because you enrolled the handset, and enrollment does not give you a casual right to erase the camera roll.\n\nThe practical design is a managed work area. Mail, files, and clinical apps live in that area. A screen lock and encryption can be required for the work area even when you do not control the whole phone. If the phone is lost or the person leaves, you remove the work area and leave personal photos, texts, and the person's own apps. That selective removal is the usual end state. A full wipe of a personal phone is a severe step that the written rule must have allowed in advance, and it is a poor default. The person should see, before they enroll, what the console can touch. Without that notice, the program is a surprise dressed up as convenience.\n\nTicket MDM-BYOD-3 covers a physician's personal phone that reads clinic mail only inside a work profile. When the physician left the group, the console removed the profile. Photos taken at a family event remained. The ticket records the device as employee-owned, the profile removal time, and a check that the camera roll was not in the removal scope. A colleague had asked the help desk to wipe the entire phone because it was faster to click. The desk refused and followed the profile removal. That refusal is the ownership model in action. The management console still did the work. The model decided the scope.\n\nDefenders write the rule before the first personal phone connects. State what data may reside on it, what the console may remove, and what happens on departure or loss. Prefer the work profile over a full-device claim. Keep personal phones off systems that cannot be containerized. Review the notice the person accepts at enrollment so it matches the console's real power. Do not quietly change a personal phone into a fully wiped device because a playbook copied from the company-owned fleet was handy.\n\nStudents hear that a phone is enrolled and answer that the company owns it. Enrollment is management. The purchase receipt is ownership. Students also treat a catalog phone the company bought, which the person was allowed to choose, as this model. That catalog choice is a different program. The separating fact is who paid for and owns the hardware. If the person owns it, and company data sits in a removable work area as on MDM-BYOD-3, you are looking at bring-your-own-device.\n",
        'r0': "Bring-your-own-device means the person owns the phone and company data still needs a rule. MDM-BYOD-3 kept mail inside a work profile on a physician's personal phone. Departure removed that profile and left the family photos. A full wipe was refused because the ownership record said employee-owned. Enrollment is not a transfer of ownership. Tell the person what the console can remove before they enroll. Systems that cannot containerize company data are a poor fit. A faster full wipe copied from the company fleet is the mistake the desk refused. Evidence is the ownership flag, the profile removal, and the personal data that remained.\n",
        'r1': 'The person owns the phone. Company data on it still needs a rule. Keep work mail in an area you can remove. Removing that area should leave personal photos. Wiping the whole phone is a poor default here. A company-owned phone is a different story. A phone picked from a company list is different too. Enrollment does not transfer ownership. Say what you will remove when the person leaves. Show that rule before the first personal phone connects. Ask to see the ownership flag. Keep family photos out of the removal.\n',
        'r2': "Picture a physician using a personal car for a clinic errand, with the clinic packages locked in a case that can be lifted out. The car stays the physician's. The packages do not. When the errand ends, you take the case and you do not scrap the car or empty the glove box of family papers. A clinic van is a different set of keys. A van the clinic bought after letting the physician pick the color is different again. In the phone program, the personal handset is that car. The work profile is the removable case, and the refused full wipe is the decision not to scrap someone else's car.\n",
        'r4': 'On MDM-BYOD-3, record that the phone is employee-owned. Record the work-profile removal and the time. Confirm personal photos were outside that removal. Do not run a full wipe because it is fewer clicks. Keep company-owned fleet steps off this ticket. Confirm the enrollment notice matches what the console can actually remove. Stop when the ticket shows personal ownership and a company-data removal that left personal data.\n',
        'exam': 'The person owns the hardware in this model. Enrollment does not transfer ownership. A removable work profile is the usual way to protect company data. A full wipe of photos is not the default. A company-owned phone is a different model. A catalog choice the organization manages is a different model. The stem to recognize is a personal phone with company data removed and personal data left in place.\n',
    },
    'Corporate-owned devices': {
        'body': "A corporate-owned device is hardware the organization bought and still owns. Because it is not the employee's property, the organization can set stricter controls and can wipe the whole device. Personal use may be allowed on some of these phones. That permission does not move the title. Exam stems often call the personally enabled version of this arrangement COPE. The ownership fact stays the same.\n\nThe management profile can refuse to install mail until a long screen lock and full-device encryption are present. The organization chooses the passcode length, the encryption requirement, and which apps may be installed. A lost phone can be wiped completely, including anything personal the user was allowed to keep there, because the hardware was never theirs. People need to know that before they store personal photos on it. Departure uses the same full control: the device comes back, or it is wiped and retired if it does not. This is stricter than a personal phone, where you usually remove only the work area. It is also distinct from letting someone pick a model. Picking a model is a choice. Ownership is the title.\n\nTicket MDM-CO-5 covers a clinic-purchased phone issued to a case manager. Mail did not appear until the console reported encryption on and a long screen lock set. When the phone was lost, the console operator wiped the whole device the same shift, and the ticket records the serial number, the wipe command, and the ownership field set to organization. A tech who suggested a profile-only removal, copied from the personal-phone procedure, was corrected. The personal-phone procedure would have been too narrow for hardware the clinic owns. The wipe is acceptable here because of that title, not because wipes are always acceptable.\n\nDefenders tag every handset with its owner in the console and make the tag drive the wipe action. Require encryption and the screen lock before data arrives, and test that the mail profile really waits. Tell users, in writing, that a company phone can be fully erased. Collect or wipe devices on the last day. Do not apply the personal-phone playbook to this fleet, and do not apply this full wipe to a phone the person bought.\n\nStudents see a managed phone and assume the company owns it. Management is the console. The invoice is the ownership. Students also treat a catalog the user may choose from as automatically this label. A catalog can be company-owned, and the question the stem must answer is still the title and the full wipe. The separating fact is ownership. MDM-CO-5 is corporate-owned because the clinic bought the phone and could erase all of it.\n",
        'r0': "A corporate-owned device is the organization's hardware, so controls can be strict and a full wipe is allowed. MDM-CO-5 withheld mail until encryption and a long screen lock were on, then wiped the whole lost phone the same shift. The ownership field said organization. COPE is the usual exam name when personal use is also allowed, and the title still sits with the organization. A work-profile-only removal is the personal-phone procedure and is too narrow here. Tell users a full erase is possible before they store personal photos. Evidence is the purchase ownership, the preconditioned mail profile, and a whole-device wipe.\n",
        'r1': "The organization owns this hardware. It can set stricter rules than on a personal phone. Require a long screen lock before mail appears. Require encryption before mail appears. A full erase is allowed because the company owns the device. Letting someone keep personal photos does not change the title. A person's own phone is a different model. Picking from a catalog is a different question. The console is how those rules are pushed. Say up front that a lost company phone can be fully erased.\n",
        'r2': "Picture a clinic van. The clinic bought it, keeps the title, and decides the locks. Drivers may keep a personal jacket in the cab if the policy allows, and they still do not own the van. When the van is stolen, the clinic can empty it completely and does not stop to sort the jacket. A physician's own car, used for one errand, is a different vehicle. Choosing the van's color from three clinic options does not make the driver the owner. In the phone fleet, the issued handset is that van. The full wipe on the lost device is emptying the cab, and the jacket was never a change of title.\n",
        'r4': 'On MDM-CO-5, record the ownership field and the serial number. Confirm mail waited for encryption and a long screen lock. Record the whole-device wipe and the time. Do not substitute a work-profile-only removal from the personal-phone procedure. Tell the user in the ticket notes that a full erase was in the rule they received. Keep catalog-choice notes separate unless the stem is about selection. Stop when the ticket shows organization ownership and a full wipe that ownership allows.\n',
        'exam': "Organization ownership is this model. A full wipe fits because the hardware is not the employee's. Encryption and a long screen lock can be required before mail. Personal use allowed on the device does not move the title. A personal phone is a different model. Choosing a model from a list is a different question. The stem to recognize is company hardware that can be erased completely.\n",
    },
    'Choose-your-own-device': {
        'body': "Choose-your-own-device lets a person pick from a list the organization already approved, and the organization still manages every choice. The freedom is the selection. It is not freedom to bring an arbitrary phone from home. If a model is not on the list, it does not enter the program.\n\nThe list exists so support, security settings, and applications are predictable. Three approved phones can be enough. Each one enrolls in management before mail or clinical apps appear. The console can push the same screen lock, encryption, and application rules to all three, with small differences the list already accounted for. Ownership is often the organization's, because the clinic buys the chosen model, but the defining fact in the stem is the limited choice plus management. A single assigned model with no choice is stricter and does not need this name. A personal phone the person already owned is the opposite program, even if you later enroll it. Letting an unlisted phone in because a director prefers it collapses the list into a slogan.\n\nTicket MDM-CYOD-2 offers three phones: two handset models from one maker and one from another. A new hire selected the third option. Mail stayed blocked until the console showed the device enrolled and encrypted. The asset record lists the model as one of the three approved identifiers. A request the same week to add a personal phone already in the hire's pocket was denied and pointed at the personal-phone program instead, which has a different removal rule. The denial belongs in the ticket. It proves the list is real. An empty approval of that pocket phone would have been bring-your-own under another name.\n\nDefenders publish the list, tie purchasing to it, and alert when an enrolled device model is not on it. Update the list through the change process when a model ages out, and do not leave a retired model silently approved. Test each approved model against the profile before you offer it, so a phone on the list can actually meet the encryption and lock rules. Train the service desk to refuse hallway additions. The management console is required here, and it is still not the name of this choice model.\n\nStudents treat any managed phone as this program because the user seems happy. Happiness is not a catalog. Students treat a personal phone under a work profile as this program because the user chose that phone. They chose it in a store, not from your list. The separating fact is the approved list plus enrollment. MDM-CYOD-2 is this model because the hire picked one of three, and an unlisted phone was refused.\n",
        'r0': "Choose-your-own-device limits the person to an approved list, and every choice is still managed. MDM-CYOD-2 offers three phone models. The hire's selection received mail only after enrollment and encryption. A personal phone from a pocket was refused and sent to the personal-ownership program. A single assigned model with no choice does not need this name. Retire models through change control. Test each listed model against the profile before you offer it. An unlisted model is a hallway addition, not a fourth approved choice. Support stays predictable only while the list stays closed. Evidence is the chosen model identifier on the approved list, plus enrollment before data.\n",
        'r1': 'The person picks from a list you already trust. Every choice still gets managed. A phone from home is not on that list. Three approved models can be enough. Mail should wait until the device is enrolled. A single assigned model is a stricter cousin. Buying the phone does not by itself define this program. The limited choice is the point. Do not add an unlisted phone in the hallway. Write down the models that are allowed. Support stays simpler when the list is short. Retire an old model on purpose.\n',
        'r2': "Picture a clinic bike rack with three bikes, each already fitted with the clinic lock. A staff member picks any of the three. None of the bikes is a personal bicycle wheeled in from home. A single assigned bike, with no choice at all, is a stricter rack and a different sign. Taking the lock off because someone prefers a private bike ends the program. In the device program, the three approved phones are those bikes. The hire's selection is the bike that was ridden only after the clinic lock was on, and the pocket phone was the private bicycle that stayed outside the rack.\n",
        'r4': 'On MDM-CYOD-2, name the three approved models and the one the hire selected. Confirm mail waited for enrollment and encryption. Record the refusal of the unlisted personal phone. Do not call a pocket phone this program. Do not call a no-choice assigned phone this program unless a list was actually offered. Keep the console procedure referenced, and keep the choice list as the fact under test. Stop when the ticket shows a listed model that enrolled before data arrived.\n',
        'exam': 'An approved list plus management is this model. Any phone from home is not. A single model with no choice is a stricter assignment. Enrollment still has to happen before mail. A happy user is not a catalog. Hallway additions delete the list. The stem to recognize is a person choosing among devices the organization already approved and still controls.\n',
    },
    'Cellular, Wi-Fi, and Bluetooth connections': {
        'body': "A phone moves among cellular service, wireless networking, and Bluetooth, and each radio has its own exposure. A policy that says the phone may have internet is unfinished. The policy has to say which of the three may carry work, and what the third one is allowed to do when it is only a headset.\n\nCellular service reaches the carrier without crossing the clinic firewall. That path is useful when the building network is down, and it is invisible to the controls you placed on the clinic wireless network. Company wireless can require a per-person sign-in and can stay off the guest path. A cafe or hotel network is a different wireless story: it is shared, it can be impersonated, and work mail should not prefer it. Bluetooth is short range. A headset profile is narrower than file transfer or a mode that accepts pairing from anyone nearby. Leaving file sharing on because a user once moved a photo is how clinic files walk to a stranger's phone in a waiting room. The three radios can be enabled independently. One internet icon on the screen hides that independence.\n\nPolicy POL-MOB-07, recorded on ticket MDM-RADIO-1, allows work mail on cellular data and on the company wireless network only. Public wireless is not in the allow. Bluetooth file transfer is forced off by the management profile, while a headset profile may remain. A test phone on a cafe network could browse the public web and could not open clinic mail. The same phone on cellular could open mail, and the console showed the cellular path as allowed. A second test with file transfer requested from a personal phone in the lobby was refused. Those three results are the policy, not a single on-off switch named wireless.\n\nDefenders push the split from the management console so a user cannot quietly re-enable file transfer. Review the company wireless name so mail is not matching a lookalike network in the cafe next door. Decide explicitly whether cellular mail is accepted, because banning it by accident stops after-hours work, and allowing it means you will not see that traffic on the clinic firewall. Revisit the policy when a new radio feature ships. Log the exceptions. A director's request to allow public wireless for a conference is a dated exception, not a silent edit.\n\nStudents treat the three connections as one network because the handset shows one status icon. The icon is a summary. Students also answer with a building wireless survey when the stem is about which phone radio may carry mail. A survey measures the building. This topic is the handset's paths. The separating fact is the radio. POL-MOB-07 is this control because cellular, company wireless, and Bluetooth file sharing are each named, allowed or refused, on MDM-RADIO-1.\n",
        'r0': 'Phones move among cellular, wireless networking, and Bluetooth, and each path needs its own allow. POL-MOB-07 on MDM-RADIO-1 permits work mail on cellular and on the company wireless network, and it forces Bluetooth file transfer off while a headset profile may stay. A cafe-network test could not open clinic mail. Cellular could. A lobby file-transfer attempt was refused. Cellular traffic skips the clinic firewall. Public wireless is easier to impersonate than the company network. Push the split from the management console. A conference request belongs in a dated exception, not in a silent edit. Evidence is a separate result for each radio, not one internet icon.\n',
        'r1': 'A phone has more than one radio. Each radio is its own decision. Cellular can carry work outside the clinic network. Company wireless can be the trusted wireless path. A cafe network is not that trusted path. Bluetooth file sharing is a short-range risk. A headset can stay while file sharing is off. Do not treat one status icon as one network. Write down which radios may carry mail. Turning a radio feature off is a real control. Review the split when a new feature appears.\n',
        'r2': 'Picture a clinic shop with three doors. The alley door opens onto the public street and never passes the front desk. The staff door opens only for people the clinic already knows. The small side door is meant for a courier handshake, not for hauling out boxes of records. Leaving all three propped open because the shop needs air is how a box walks away. Painting one word, open, above all three hides the differences. In the phone policy, cellular service is the alley, company wireless is the staff door, and Bluetooth file sharing is the side door you keep shut. The headset that may remain is a courier handshake, not the box of records.\n',
        'r4': 'Open MDM-RADIO-1 and quote POL-MOB-07 for each radio. Record that mail is allowed on cellular and on company wireless. Record that public wireless is excluded and that Bluetooth file transfer is off. Note the cafe test and the lobby file-transfer refusal. Do not collapse the three results into one wireless word. Do not replace this ticket with a building coverage walk. Stop when each path has its own allow or refusal.\n',
        'exam': 'Three radios mean three decisions. Cellular skips the clinic firewall. Company wireless is not cafe wireless. Bluetooth file sharing is not a headset. One status icon does not merge the paths. A building coverage survey is a different topic. The stem to recognize is a policy that allows or refuses each connection separately, including a file-sharing path that stays off.\n',
    },
}
